 \textbf{5. Sweeping Reach to Quasi-Judicial Duties.}
 In sweeping language that would ignite a century-long battle over independent agencies, Taft declared that even when executive officers or tribunals perform ``duties of a quasi judicial character,'' although the President cannot dictate a particular decision, he ``may consider the decision after its rendition as a reason for removing the officer'' to fulfill his Take Care duty.
 
-\section{Concurring and Dissenting Opinions}
-All three dissenters wrote separately, forming an unusual coalition spanning the Court's conservative and progressive wings.
+\subsection{Dissenting Opinion (Justice Holmes)}
 
 \textbf{Justice Holmes, dissenting} (272 U.S.\ at 177): In a terse, two-paragraph dissent, Holmes dismissed Taft's structural deductions as ``spider's webs inadequate to control the dominant facts.'' Because Congress creates the office of postmaster, fixes its duration and pay, and may abolish it tomorrow, Congress can attach reasonable conditions to removal. The President's duty to take care that the laws be faithfully executed ``means no more than that the President must enforce the acts of Congress''---including the 1876 Act's removal restriction.
 
+\subsection{Dissenting Opinion (Justice McReynolds)}
+
 \textbf{Justice McReynolds, dissenting} (272 U.S.\ at 178): In an exhaustive dissent, McReynolds argued that the Constitution contains no express grant of an illimitable removal power and that inferring one from the general phrase ``executive Power'' violates the principle of enumerated powers. Distinguishing execution from legislation, he maintained that while the actual ouster of an officer is executive, prescribing the statutory conditions of tenure for an inferior office created by Congress is legislative. He also disputed Taft's reading of the 1789 debates, noting that only a minority of House speakers endorsed an indefeasible constitutional removal power and warning that Taft's broad theory threatened the independence of the ICC, FTC, Federal Reserve Board, and statutory courts.
 
+\subsection{Dissenting Opinion (Justice Brandeis)}
+
 \textbf{Justice Brandeis, dissenting} (272 U.S.\ at 240): Reviewing 137 years of federal statutes and state constitutional practice, Brandeis demonstrated that Congress had repeatedly restricted the removal of inferior officers with presidential approval and that the Decision of 1789 addressed only the removal of high-level department heads serving at pleasure, not inferior officers appointed for fixed terms. Rejecting Taft's claim that executive efficiency demanded unchecked presidential firing power, Brandeis delivered his celebrated defense of constitutional checks and balances: the separation of powers was adopted ``not to promote efficiency but to preclude the exercise of arbitrary power.''
 
+\subsection{Subsequent Developments}
+
+\begin{itemize}
+
+\item \textbf{1. Retrenchment in the New Deal and Mid-Twentieth Century (1935--1988).}
+Just nine years after \textit{Myers}, a unanimous Court sharply curtailed Taft's opinion in \textit{Humphrey's Executor v.\ United States}, 295 U.S.\ 602 (1935), upholding statutory ``for-cause'' removal protections (``inefficiency, neglect of duty, or malfeasance in office'') for Federal Trade Commission (FTC) Commissioners. Writing for the Court, Justice Sutherland (who had joined Taft's majority in \textit{Myers}) confined \textit{Myers} to ``purely executive officers'' like postmasters and disapproved its broader statements as \textit{dicta}, characterizing the FTC as a non-executive, ``quasi-legislative'' and ``quasi-judicial'' body. Subsequent decisions built on this functionalist turn: \textit{Wiener v.\ United States}, 357 U.S.\ 349 (1958) (implying for-cause protection for the adjudicatory War Claims Commission), and \textit{Morrison v.\ Olson}, 487 U.S.\ 654 (1988) (upholding for-cause protection for an inferior-officer independent counsel under a functional ``undue interference'' test, over Justice Scalia's famous lone dissent). Even during this era, however, \textit{Myers}'s core prohibition against \emph{congressional participation} in removal remained bedrock law, reaffirmed in \textit{Bowsher v.\ Synar}, 478 U.S.\ 714 (1986).
+
+\item \textbf{2. The Roberts Court's Revival of \textit{Myers} (2010--2020).}
+Beginning in \textit{Free Enterprise Fund v.\ Public Company Accounting Oversight Board}, 561 U.S.\ 477 (2010) (invalidating dual-layer for-cause removal protections for PCAOB members), and accelerating in \textit{Seila Law LLC v.\ Consumer Financial Protection Bureau}, 591 U.S.\ 197 (2020) (invalidating for-cause removal protection for the single director of the CFPB), the Roberts Court restored \textit{Myers} as the baseline constitutional rule: the President's ``general administrative control'' includes an unrestricted default removal power, and \textit{Humphrey's Executor} was treated as a narrow, historically anomalous exception not to be extended to new agency designs.
+
+\item \textbf{3. Centennial Vindication: \textit{Trump v.\ Slaughter}, 609 U.S.\ \_\_\_ (2026).}
+Exactly one century after \textit{Myers}, the Supreme Court completed its full doctrinal vindication in \textit{Trump v.\ Slaughter}, 609 U.S.\ \_\_\_ (No.\ 25--332, decided June 29, 2026). After President Donald J.\ Trump dismissed Democratic FTC Commissioners Rebecca Slaughter and Alvaro Bedoya in March 2025 without alleging statutory cause, the Court reversed the D.C.\ District Court in a 6--3 decision authored by Chief Justice Roberts (joined in full by Justices Alito, Gorsuch, Kavanaugh, and Barrett, and joined by Justice Thomas as to all but Part III--B). Opening with an explicit tribute---``One hundred years ago, this Court honored the Convention's choice in the seminal case of \textit{Myers v.\ United States}, 272 U.S.\ 52 (1926)''---the Court formally overruled \textit{Humphrey's Executor} and invalidated the FTC's for-cause removal statute (15 U.S.C.\ \S~41). Holding that the FTC's sweeping rulemaking, in-house adjudication, and federal-court enforcement powers are ``quintessentially executive,'' Roberts reaffirmed that all principal officers exercising executive power must be removable by the President at will to preserve democratic accountability through Madison's ``chain of dependence.''
+
+\end{itemize}
+
+\section{Examples: Future Applications}
+\begin{enumerate}
+
+\item \textbf{Same-Side (Removal Restriction Invalid): For-Cause Protection for the Commissioners of a Federal AI Commission.} Congress enacts the Artificial Intelligence Safety Act of 2026, establishing the five-member Federal AI Commission (FAIC), appointed by the President with Senate confirmation for staggered seven-year terms, with no more than three members from the same political party. The FAIC is empowered to promulgate binding safety rules for frontier AI models, investigate technology firms, adjudicate violations in-house, and seek billion-dollar civil penalties in federal court. The statute provides two removal clauses: \textbf{Clause 1:} ``Commissioners may be removed by the President only for inefficiency, neglect of duty, or malfeasance in office.'' \textbf{Clause 2:} ``The FAIC's Chief Inspector---an inferior officer appointed by the FAIC Chair---may be removed by the Chair only upon a showing of good cause.'' The President fires two FAIC Commissioners solely for policy disagreements and orders the new Chair to fire the Chief Inspector at will. Both the Commissioners and the Chief Inspector sue.
+
+Under \textit{Myers v.\ United States} as fully vindicated by \textit{Trump v.\ Slaughter} (2026), \textbf{Clause 1 is plainly unconstitutional}: the FAIC wields quintessential executive powers (binding rulemaking, investigations, in-house adjudication, and civil-penalty litigation), so its principal officers must be removable by the President at will, and \textit{Humphrey's Executor} is no longer good law. Nor can the FAIC claim the \textit{Trump v.\ Cook} historical carve-out applicable to the Federal Reserve.
+
+\item \textbf{Same-Side (Removal Restriction Invalid): Congress Reserves Removal to Itself.} A statute creates a Comptroller of Federal Spending who certifies the amount of automatic budget cuts that the President must then order, and provides that the Comptroller may be removed only by joint resolution of Congress. Even on the narrow reading of \textit{Myers} adopted in \textit{Humphrey's Executor}, its core prohibition on congressional participation in removal survives: Congress may not draw to itself, or to either House, a power to remove an officer charged with executing the laws. \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/478/714/}{\textit{Bowsher v.\ Synar}, 478 U.S.\ 714 (1986)}, invalidated a materially identical arrangement on exactly this ground, because Congress's control over removal would make the officer its subordinate.
+
+\item \textbf{Opposite-Side (Restriction Likely Valid): An Inferior Officer Appointed and Protected by a Department Head.} Congress vests the appointment of a naval engineering officer in the Secretary of the Navy and provides that the officer may be dismissed in peacetime only by sentence of a court-martial; the Secretary serves at the President's pleasure. \textit{Myers} itself distinguished this situation: when Congress vests an inferior officer's appointment in a head of department under the Excepting Clause, it may limit that department head's power to remove, as \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/116/483/}{\textit{United States v.\ Perkins}, 116 U.S.\ 483 (1886)}, held. Congress claims no share of removal for itself or the Senate, and the President still controls the Secretary, so the officer's tenure protection stands.
+
+\item \textbf{Opposite-Side (Myers Not Triggered): Civil-Service Protection for a Non-Officer Employee.} A claims examiner in a federal agency, hired through competitive civil-service procedures, applies agency guidelines to routine benefit claims, exercises no significant authority under federal law, and by statute may be dismissed only for misconduct or poor performance after notice and a hearing before a personnel board. \textit{Myers} concerns the removal of \emph{officers} through whom the President executes the laws; an ordinary employee is not an officer under \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/424/1/}{\textit{Buckley v.\ Valeo}, 424 U.S.\ 1 (1976)}, and \textit{Free Enterprise Fund} expressly declined to cast doubt on the civil-service system. The President's control runs through the agency's officers, who remain answerable to him, so the examiner's protection is valid.
+
+\item \textbf{Fence-Sitter (Unresolved): Single-Layer Protection for the Commission's Chief Inspector.} Return to the Federal AI Commission and its Chief Inspector under Clause 2. As to \textbf{Clause 2}, the Chief Inspector is an inferior officer whose appointment Congress vested in a Head of Department (the Commission/Chair) under Article II, \S~2's Excepting Clause. Under \textit{Myers}'s own discussion of \textit{United States v.\ Perkins}, 116 U.S.\ 483 (1886) (and \textit{Morrison v.\ Olson}), Congress may impose a \emph{single} layer of for-cause protection on an inferior officer appointed by a department head, provided the department head is removable at will by the President (avoiding the unconstitutional \emph{dual-layer} tenure problem of \textit{Free Enterprise Fund}). Once Clause 1 is severed under \textit{Slaughter}, the Chair is removable at will, meaning Clause 2 creates only a single layer of protection---though challengers could still argue under a maximalist reading of \textit{Myers} and \textit{Slaughter} that even single-layer limits on inferior officers exercising core enforcement power impair the President's Take Care duty.
+
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\section{Critique}
+\textbf{Originalist Contestability of the Decision of 1789:} Modern legal historians (cited extensively by Justice Sotomayor in her \textit{Slaughter} dissent) argue that Taft overstated the consensus of the First Congress: the 1789 House was fractured into three or four competing factions, and its vote rejecting \emph{Senate participation} in removal did not necessarily resolve whether Congress could impose \emph{for-cause} tenure limits via statute.
+
+\textbf{The Ratchet Effect of Unitary Removal Without Nondelegation:} As Justice Gorsuch highlighted in \textit{Trump v.\ Slaughter} (2026), combining \textit{Myers}'s plenary presidential removal power with twentieth-century broad delegations of rulemaking and adjudicative authority concentrates unprecedented power in a single executive---creating an institutional tension that neither Taft in 1926 nor the \textit{Slaughter} majority fully resolved.
+
