-\section{Concurring and Dissenting Opinions}
-\textbf{Justice Cardozo, concurring (joined by Justice Stone):} Having been the lone dissenter in \textit{Panama Refining} just four months earlier, Cardozo wrote separately to explain why NIRA \S\,3 crossed the constitutional line where \S\,9(c) had not. In \textit{Panama Refining}, Congress had conferred discretion only over the \textit{occasion} for prohibiting a single, defined act (transporting excess oil), not a ``roving commission.'' Here, by contrast, the power was ``not canalized within banks that keep it from overflowing''; it was ``unconfined and vagrant''---a ``roving commission to inquire into evils and, upon discovery, correct them'' (295 U.S.\ at 551). Because ``codes of fair competition'' encompassed positive economic planning rather than merely suppressing recognized unfair trade practices, Cardozo famously declared: ``This is delegation running riot'' (295 U.S.\ at 553). On the Commerce Clause, Cardozo acknowledged Judge Learned Hand's observation below that society is an ``elastic medium which transmits all tremors throughout its territory,'' but insisted that ``[t]he law is not indifferent to considerations of degree''; local activities do not become interstate merely through distant repercussions, lest ``there will be an end to our federal system'' (295 U.S.\ at 554).
