   \item \textbf{Jurisdictional Findings and Non-Abatement:} Exercising appellate jurisdiction to reach the alternative demurrer grounds, the Court held that the President's recital that he had consulted other American Republics and secured necessary cooperation tracked the Resolution and was sufficient. Finally, the November 1935 proclamation merely ended the factual condition that triggered the embargo prospectively; it did not repeal the underlying statute or abate prosecutions for crimes committed between May 28, 1934, and November 14, 1935.
 \end{enumerate}
 
-\section{Concurring and Dissenting Opinions}
-\textbf{Justice McReynolds, dissenting:} Justice McReynolds issued a two-sentence dissent without a written opinion, stating simply that he disagreed and believed the District Court had reached the right conclusion in holding the delegation unconstitutional and ought to be affirmed (299 U.S.\ at 333). (Justice Stone did not participate.)
+\subsection{Dissenting Opinion (Justice McReynolds)}
+
+Justice McReynolds issued a two-sentence dissent without a written opinion, stating simply that he disagreed and believed the District Court had reached the right conclusion in holding the delegation unconstitutional and ought to be affirmed (299 U.S.\ at 333). (Justice Stone did not participate.)
+
+\subsection{Subsequent Developments}
+
+\begin{itemize}
+
+\item \textbf{\textit{Youngstown Sheet \& Tube Co. v. Sawyer}, 343 U.S.\ 579 (1952):} In his canonical tripartite concurrence, Justice Jackson reined in \textit{Curtiss-Wright} by classifying it as a \textbf{Zone~1} case (where the President acts with express congressional authorization) and dismissing much of Sutherland's expansive language as dicta. Jackson emphasized that \textit{Curtiss-Wright} involved ``the right of the President to act under and in accord with an Act of Congress,'' not a license to defy Congress or exercise unchecked domestic power under a foreign-affairs label (343 U.S.\ at 635--636 n.\,2).
+
+\item \textbf{\textit{Zivotofsky v. Kerry}, 576 U.S.\ 1 (2015)} (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/576/1/}{Justia}): Although the Court held (6--3) that the President possesses the exclusive power to recognize foreign sovereigns---invalidating a statute requiring ``Israel'' to be listed on passports of citizens born in Jerusalem---Justice Kennedy's majority opinion \textbf{expressly repudiated} \textit{Curtiss-Wright}'s broad ``sole organ'' dicta. The Court explained that \textit{Curtiss-Wright} addressed a congressional delegation and declined to endorse an unbounded, extraconstitutional presidential monopoly over foreign policy, grounding the recognition power instead in Article~II's Reception Clause (\S\,3) and functional unity.
+
+\item \textbf{\textit{Learning Resources, Inc. v. Trump}, 607 U.S.\ 229 (2026)} (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287/}{Justia}; \href{https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/24-1287_4gcj.pdf}{Slip Op.}): \textit{Curtiss-Wright} took center stage in the Court's 6--3 invalidation of President Trump's tariffs imposed under IEEPA. Chief Justice Roberts's plurality (joined by Gorsuch and Barrett, JJ.) rejected the Government's and Justice Kavanaugh's proposed ``foreign affairs exception'' to the major questions doctrine, holding that peacetime tariffs belong to ``Congress alone'' under Article~I, \S\,8, cl.\ 1, rather than to concurrent or independent Article~II authority. Justice Gorsuch's concurrence explicitly cabined \textit{Curtiss-Wright} to its narrow statutory context---a targeted arms embargo on two warring nations conditioned on consultation and factual findings---refusing to read it as allowing Congress to hand off its core taxing and foreign-commerce powers through vague text. By contrast, Justice Kavanaugh's principal dissent (joined by Thomas and Alito, JJ.) and Justice Thomas's separate dissent leaned heavily on \textit{Curtiss-Wright} (299 U.S.\ at 315, 319, 321--322, 324) to argue that foreign-commerce and tariff delegations lie outside domestic major-questions skepticism and nondelegation constraints.
+
+\end{itemize}
+
+\section{Examples: Future Applications}
+\begin{enumerate}
+
+\item \textbf{Same-Side (Delegation Upheld): A Presidential Ban on Advanced-Chip Exports.} Suppose Congress passes the ``Global Semiconductor Security Act,'' authorizing the President, upon declaring a ``geopolitical supply-chain threat,'' to ``prohibit, license, or impose financial conditions on the export of advanced chips to any foreign nation if the President determines such action will advance U.S.\ strategic stability.'' Invoking the Act, the President issues a proclamation banning chip exports to Country~X and imposing a 20\% export fee on chips shipped to Country~Y, without consulting Congress or holding hearings. A U.S.\ chipmaker indicted for exporting to Country~X and assessed the fee on shipments to Country~Y challenges both measures. As to the export ban: Strong \textit{Curtiss-Wright} Zone~1 territory. Like the 1934 Chaco arms embargo, the statute targets a defined class of strategic goods in external relations, requires a presidential finding tied to foreign strategic stability, and implicates confidential national-security assessments. Under \textit{Curtiss-Wright} and Justice Kavanaugh's concurrence in \textit{Consumers' Research} (2025), a nondelegation challenge to the export ban will almost certainly fail.
+
+\item \textbf{Same-Side (Delegation Upheld): Sanctions on a Foreign Regime That Attacks Its Neighbor.} Congress authorizes the President, upon finding that a foreign government has invaded a neighboring state, to prohibit U.S.\ persons from selling aircraft parts to that government, subject to such exceptions as the President prescribes, and to lift the ban when he finds hostilities have ended. The President activates the ban, and an exporter prosecuted for a later shipment argues that Congress gave the President unbounded discretion. The statute is a near copy of the 1934 Joint Resolution: it addresses a situation ``entirely external to the United States,'' turns on diplomatic judgments best made by the Executive, and follows a long tradition of embargo statutes. Under \textit{Curtiss-Wright}, the breadth of the President's discretion is no constitutional defect, and the prosecution proceeds.
+
+\item \textbf{Opposite-Side (Delegation Does Not Save the Measure): An Export Fee Barred by the Constitution.} Same statute, but the chipmaker challenges the 20\% fee on its shipments to Country~Y. Dramatically weaker for the President. First, even under \textit{Curtiss-Wright}, Sutherland expressly stated that executive foreign-affairs power ``must be exercised in subordination to the applicable provisions of the Constitution'' (299 U.S.\ at 320)---and the Export Clause (Art.\ I, \S\,9, cl.\ 5) flatly forbids taxes or duties on exports (a constitutional defect Chief Justice Roberts highlighted in \textit{Learning Resources} (2026)). Second, even if characterized as a regulatory condition rather than a tax, the chipmaker will invoke \textit{Learning Resources} (2026) and \textit{Zivotofsky} (2015) to argue that ``financial conditions'' does not clearly authorize revenue-raising exactions and that \textit{Curtiss-Wright} cannot override explicit Article~I limits.
+
+\item \textbf{Opposite-Side (No Statute, No ``Sole Organ'' Power): A Presidential Order Binding State Courts.} After an international tribunal rules that the United States violated a treaty in a state criminal prosecution, the President, acting without any statute, issues a memorandum directing state courts to reopen the affected convictions. The treaty is not self-executing, and Congress has passed no implementing law. The Executive cites \textit{Curtiss-Wright}'s description of the President as ``sole organ'' in foreign affairs. That argument fails: \textit{Curtiss-Wright} upheld presidential action taken \emph{under} an Act of Congress, and Justice Jackson's \textit{Youngstown} concurrence treats it as a case of maximum, congressionally authorized power. On materially similar facts, \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/552/491/}{\textit{Medell\'in v. Texas}, 552 U.S. 491 (2008)}, held that the President could not unilaterally convert a non-self-executing treaty obligation into domestic law binding state courts.
+
+\item \textbf{Fence-Sitter (Foreign Threat, Domestic Effects): Banning a Foreign-Owned App.} Congress authorizes the President, upon finding that a foreign-controlled software application poses a national-security threat, to prohibit its distribution in the United States on such terms as the President deems appropriate. The President bans a popular foreign-owned messaging app used daily by millions of Americans. The case for \textit{Curtiss-Wright} deference is real: the finding rests on confidential intelligence about a foreign government, a matter in which the Executive has the confidential information, secrecy, and dispatch that Sutherland stressed. But the ban operates almost entirely on domestic commerce and domestic speech, which pushes it toward the internal sphere where \textit{Panama Refining} and \textit{Schechter} demand meaningful statutory standards, and Sutherland himself said foreign-affairs power remains subject to the Constitution's other provisions, including the First Amendment. Whether a court treats the delegation as external or internal---and how much weight it gives the speech interests---could decide the case.
+
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\section{Critique}
+\begin{itemize}
+  \item \textit{Flawed Historical Narrative:} Constitutional historians (notably Charles Lofgren, \textit{83 Yale L.J.\ 1 (1973)}, and David Levitan, \textit{55 Yale L.J.\ 467 (1946)}) have thoroughly critiqued Sutherland's ``extraconstitutional sovereignty'' thesis. Between 1776 and 1789, individual States frequently exercised external attributes (maintaining navies, imposing tariffs, and negotiating border agreements), and the Constitution itself carefully \textit{divides} foreign-affairs powers between Congress (declaring war, regulating foreign commerce, raising armies, funding diplomacy) and the President (Commander in Chief, negotiating treaties with Senate consent, receiving ambassadors).
+  \item \textit{Misquoting John Marshall's ``Sole Organ'' Speech:} When Representative Marshall called the President the ``sole organ of the nation in its external relations'' in 1800, he was defending President Adams's execution of an existing extradition treaty---describing the President as the sole \textit{communicator} of national policy already established by law or treaty, not as the sole \textit{maker} of foreign policy. Sutherland transformed Marshall's modest conduit metaphor into a sweeping claim of substantive executive exclusivity.
+  \item \textit{Logical Leap from Federal Power to Presidential Power:} Even if external sovereignty passed directly from the Crown to the United States as a whole, that historical premise explains why \textit{the federal government} (as against the States) holds plenary foreign-affairs power---it does not explain why that power belongs to \textit{the President} rather than \textit{Congress}.
+\end{itemize}
 
