-\section{Doctrinal Significance and Modern Legacy (Through 2026)}
-Although the government won the judgment in \textit{Gundy}, the decision initially appeared to put the modern nondelegation doctrine on life support: four Justices (Gorsuch, Roberts, Thomas, and Alito) expressed readiness to reconsider the post-1935 intelligible-principle framework, and five months later Justice Kavanaugh---who had been recused in \textit{Gundy}---issued a statement respecting the denial of certiorari in \textit{Paul v. United States}, 589 U.S. \_\_\_ (Nov. 25, 2019), praising Justice Gorsuch's ``scholarly analysis'' as warranting ``further consideration in future cases'' (\url{https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/19pdf/17-8830_5hdk.pdf}).
-
-Yet between 2020 and 2026, the predicted constitutional revolution took a markedly different trajectory---vindicating Justice Gorsuch's ``hydraulic pressures'' observation while preserving the intelligible-principle test on the books:
-\begin{itemize}
-  \item \textbf{Indirect Policing via Statutory Canons (2022--2024):} Rather than invalidating statutes under Article I, the Court aggressively constrained agency lawmaking by elevating the Major Questions Doctrine in \textit{West Virginia v. EPA}, 597 U.S. 697 (2022), and \textit{Biden v. Nebraska}, 600 U.S. 477 (2023), and by overruling \textit{Chevron} deference in \textit{Loper Bright Enterprises v. Raimondo}, 603 U.S. 369 (2024). (In \textit{Allstates Refractory Contractors, LLC v. Su}, July 2, 2024, Justice Thomas dissented from denial of certiorari, unsuccessfully urging the Court to use OSHA's delegation to revisit \textit{Gundy}; \url{https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/23pdf/23-819_m648.pdf}.)
-  \item \textbf{Reaffirmation of the Intelligible Principle Test in \textit{FCC v. Consumers' Research}, 606 U.S. \_\_\_ (2025):} On June 27, 2025, the Court confronted its first full-dress nondelegation merits case since \textit{Gundy} and decisively rejected the anticipated revival, upholding the FCC's Universal Service Fund (USF) contribution scheme under 47 U.S.C. \S~254 by a 6--3 vote (\url{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/606/24-354/}). Justice Kagan---joined by Chief Justice Roberts and Justices Sotomayor, Kavanaugh, Barrett, and Jackson---reaffirmed the ``intelligible principle'' test, rejected a stricter numeric-cap requirement for revenue-raising delegations (following \textit{J.W. Hampton} and \textit{Skinner v. Mid-America Pipeline Co.}, 490 U.S. 212 (1989)), held that ``sufficient'' funding coupled with \S~254's mandatory service criteria constrained the FCC, rejected the private nondelegation challenge to the Universal Service Administrative Company under \textit{Sunshine Anthracite Coal Co. v. Adkins}, 310 U.S. 381 (1940), and rejected the en banc Fifth Circuit's ``combination'' theory. Crucially, Justice Kavanaugh's concurrence explained why the intelligible-principle test endures: \textit{Loper Bright} and the major questions doctrine already check domestic agency overreach, while broad delegations remain vital in foreign affairs and national security; the real accountability threat, he argued, comes from delegations to \textit{independent} agencies insulated from presidential removal. Justice Gorsuch (joined by Justices Thomas and Alito) dissented, repeating his \textit{Gundy} critique that the majority rewrote \S~254's text to save an unprecedented delegation of the taxing power (while noting the majority's footnote 9 refusal to bless \S\S~254(c)(3) and (h)(2)).
-  \item \textbf{2026 Consolidation --- Statutory Restraint and Unitary Executive Accountability:} Two blockbuster 2026 decisions cemented the post-\textit{Gundy} equilibrium. First, in \textit{Learning Resources, Inc. v. Trump}, 607 U.S. \_\_\_ (Feb. 20, 2026), the Court struck down the President's IEEPA tariffs 6--3 on statutory grounds---with Chief Justice Roberts and Justices Gorsuch and Barrett invoking the major questions doctrine---rather than relying on constitutional nondelegation (\url{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287/}). Second, in \textit{Trump v. Slaughter}, 609 U.S. \_\_\_ (June 29, 2026), the Court overruled \textit{Humphrey's Executor v. United States}, 295 U.S. 602 (1935) and invalidated statutory for-cause removal protections for FTC commissioners (\url{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/609/25-332/})---adopting the first of Justice Kavanaugh's two Article II remedies from \textit{Consumers' Research} by restoring presidential removal control over agencies rather than dismantling Congress's ability to delegate under the intelligible-principle test.
