-\textbf{Exam Hypothetical (and ``Opposite Side'' Contrast):} \\
-\textit{Hypothetical:} Responding to rapid developments in autonomous vehicles and artificial intelligence, Congress enacts the ``National AI Safety Act of 2026.'' Section 4 of the Act empowers the Secretary of Commerce ``to promulgate such rules, licensing conditions, and criminal prohibitions (punishable by up to five years' imprisonment) governing the training and deployment of frontier artificial intelligence models as the Secretary determines are necessary and appropriate to protect the public interest, promote American innovation, and safeguard national security.'' The statute contains no further definition of ``frontier'' models, no threshold metrics, and no list of prohibited practices. Relying on Section 4, the Secretary issues a rule making it a federal felony to release any open-weight AI model trained using more than $10^{25}$ floating-point operations without a federal license. A software developer indicted under the rule challenges Section 4 under the nondelegation doctrine.
-
-\textit{Analysis \& ``Opposite Side'' Contrast:}
-\begin{itemize}
-  \item \textit{Challenger's Argument (Under Gorsuch's \textit{Gundy} Dissent and the Sliding Scale of \textit{Whitman} / \textit{Consumers' Research}):} Unlike SORNA's transitional ``as soon as feasible'' mandate in \textit{Gundy} or the bounded beneficiary and service criteria in \textit{FCC v. Consumers' Research}, Section 4 confers economy-wide criminal lawmaking power with zero determinate boundaries. Even under the majority's intelligible-principle test in \textit{Consumers' Research}, ``the degree of agency discretion that is acceptable varies according to the scope of the power congressionally conferred'' (\textit{Whitman v. American Trucking Assns., Inc.}, 531 U.S. 457, 475 (2001)). Combining the ``public interest'' with competing goals (``innovation'' vs. ``safety'') without statutory definitions replicates the ``preface of generalities'' struck down in \textit{Schechter Poultry}. Moreover, under the Major Questions Doctrine (\textit{West Virginia v. EPA}; \textit{Learning Resources, Inc. v. Trump}), Congress does not hide an economy-reshaping criminal ban on open-source software in vague boilerplate.
-  \item \textbf{Opposite Side Contrast (Government's Defense under Kagan's \textit{Gundy} Plurality and Kavanaugh's \textit{Consumers' Research} Concurrence):} The government will invoke the Court's unbroken post-1935 practice of upholding ``public interest'' standards (\textit{NBC v. United States}; \textit{Yakus}) and argue that ``frontier'' AI and ``national security'' supply contextual boundaries---especially because, as Justice Kavanaugh emphasized in \textit{Consumers' Research} (and Justice Gorsuch acknowledged in his third \textit{Gundy} category), nondelegation and the major questions doctrine play a far more limited role when Congress delegates to the President or an accountable Cabinet Secretary in the \textit{national security} realm. Furthermore, unlike an independent agency, the Secretary of Commerce is removable at will by the President, mitigating Article II accountability concerns. Finally, the government will urge the Court to apply \textit{Gundy}'s statutory-interpretation-first method by construing ``national security'' and ``frontier'' narrowly to cover only demonstrable dual-use biological or cyber weapons risks, thereby saving Section 4 from facial invalidation.
-\end{itemize}
 
