-\section{Pedagogical Critiques and Exam Hypotheticals}
-\textbf{Pedagogical Critiques:}
-\begin{itemize}
-  \item \textbf{Substantive Canon vs. Contextual Textualism:} The Gorsuch--Barrett--Kagan debate across \textit{West Virginia}, \textit{Nebraska}, and \textit{Learning Resources} is a premier teaching vehicle for statutory interpretation: if a court demands ``clear congressional authorization'' even when a broad term (``system,'' ``modify,'' ``regulate'') textually covers the action, is the Court faithfully reading Congress's text or enforcing an unenumerated, judicially managed constitutional avoidance rule?
-  \item \textbf{Malleability of ``Majorness'' and Partisan Neutrality:} Critics initially charged that the major questions doctrine was a one-way deregulatory ratchet aimed at Democratic administrations (\textit{West Virginia}, \textit{NFIB}, \textit{Nebraska}). \textit{Learning Resources v. Trump} (2026) directly tests that critique---showing Roberts, Gorsuch, and Barrett applying the doctrine to strike down a Republican President's signature tariff policy, while Kavanaugh, Thomas, and Alito dissented.
-  \item \textbf{The ``Failed Legislation'' Trap:} Justice Kagan's critique of relying on failed subsequent bills highlights a classic textualist tension: congressional inaction can reflect filibuster gridlock or belief that the agency \textit{already} possesses statutory authority, not repudiation of an earlier Congress's enacted text.
-\end{itemize}
-
-\textbf{Exam Hypothetical:}
-In 1974, Congress enacted the Federal Biosecurity Preparedness Act, authorizing the Secretary of Health and Human Services to ``promulgate such standards and systems as the Secretary determines necessary to prevent catastrophic biological threats.'' For fifty years, HHS used the statute only to set lab-containment protocols for federal research facilities. Following a major public debate in which Congress repeatedly failed to pass a bill regulating artificial intelligence, the Secretary invokes the 1974 Act to issue a rule requiring every U.S. technology company training a frontier AI model above a specified compute threshold to obtain a federal license and submit model weights to HHS for biosecurity red-teaming, at an estimated industry compliance cost of \$18 billion per year.
-\begin{itemize}
-  \item \textbf{Major Questions Analysis (\textit{West Virginia} / \textit{Nebraska} / \textit{Learning Resources}):} Challengers have a textbook major-questions claim: (1) an unheralded, transformative expansion of authority discovered in a 50-year-old statute used only for physical labs; (2) vast economic (\$18 billion/year) and political significance; (3) a stark mismatch between HHS's public-health mission and comprehensive licensing of the frontier AI sector; and (4) conspicuous congressional failure to enact AI licensing legislation. Under Roberts/Gorsuch, ``standards and systems'' is an ``empty vessel'' lacking clear congressional authorization; under Barrett/Kagan, even ordinary text-in-context interpretation makes it implausible that Congress hid a national AI-licensing regime inside a 1974 lab-safety clause.
-  \item \textbf{``Opposite Side'' Contrast:} Now alter the facts: suppose Congress in 2025 enacted the Artificial Intelligence Safety Act expressly granting a newly created AI Safety Agency authority to ``license and audit frontier AI models exceeding $10^{26}$ FLOPs to mitigate chemical, biological, radiological, or cyber risks,'' and the Agency sets detailed red-teaming standards under that statute. Here, the \textit{major questions doctrine does not defeat the rule}: Congress spoke clearly, the action sits squarely in the agency's core mission and expertise, and the statute is contemporaneous rather than an old ``backwater.'' Any challenge must therefore shift from statutory major-questions review to a constitutional \textit{nondelegation} claim---which, after \textit{FCC v. Consumers' Research}, 606 U.S. 656 (2025), will fail so long as Congress supplied an ``intelligible principle'' setting the general policy and boundaries of delegated authority.
-\end{itemize}
 
