-\subsection{Two-Tier Framework and Pre-2023 Evolution}
-Paired with \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/437/617/}{\textit{City of Philadelphia v. New Jersey}, 437 U.S. 617 (1978)}, \textit{Pike} cemented the two-tier Dormant Commerce Clause: discriminatory laws face ``virtually per se'' invalidity, while evenhanded laws with incidental effects undergo \textit{Pike} balancing. In practice, the Court rarely struck down nondiscriminatory regulations, upholding market-structure and packaging rules in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/437/117/}{\textit{Exxon Corp. v. Governor of Maryland}, 437 U.S. 117, 127 (1978)} (protecting the interstate market, not particular firms' ``methods of operation''), and \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/449/456/}{\textit{Minnesota v. Clover Leaf Creamery Co.}, 449 U.S. 456 (1981)}. Rare invalidations---such as \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/450/662/}{\textit{Kassel v. Consolidated Freightways Corp.}, 450 U.S. 662 (1981)}, and \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/457/624/}{\textit{Edgar v. MITE Corp.}, 457 U.S. 624 (1982)}---involved interstate transportation or direct out-of-state tender-offer regulation. Meanwhile, Justice Scalia attacked \textit{Pike} as weighing ``whether a particular line is longer than a particular rock is heavy'' (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/486/888/}{\textit{Bendix Autolite Corp. v. Midwesco Enters.}, 486 U.S. 888, 897 (1988)}), and \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/06-666}{\textit{Dept. of Revenue of Ky. v. Davis}, 553 U.S. 328, 353--355 (2008)}, warned of judicial institutional limits.
