+
+\textbf{Formalism vs. Practical Burden:} Modern doctrine asks \textit{how} Congress regulates (general rules vs. commandeering) rather than \textit{what} function is regulated, even though generally applicable labor mandates (\textit{Garcia}) can burden state budgets far more heavily than minor executive duties (\textit{Printz}).
+
+\section{Key Quotations}
+\begin{quote}
+``We therefore now reject, as unsound in principle and unworkable in practice, a rule of state immunity from federal regulation that turns on a judicial appraisal of whether a particular governmental function is `integral' or `traditional.' Any such rule leads to inconsistent results at the same time that it disserves principles of democratic self-governance, and it breeds inconsistency precisely because it is divorced from those principles.''
+\end{quote}
+\noindent --- Blackmun, J., for the Court, \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/469/528/}{469 U.S. at 546--547}.
+
+\begin{quote}
+``Apart from the limitation on federal authority inherent in the delegated nature of Congress' Article I powers, the principal means chosen by the Framers to ensure the role of the States in the federal system lies in the structure of the Federal Government itself.''
+\end{quote}
+\noindent --- Blackmun, J., for the Court, 469 U.S.\ at 550.
+
+\begin{quote}
+``State sovereign interests, then, are more properly protected by procedural safeguards inherent in the structure of the federal system than by judicially created limitations on federal power.''
+\end{quote}
+\noindent --- Blackmun, J., for the Court, 469 U.S.\ at 552.
+
+\begin{quote}
+``Nonetheless, against this background, we are convinced that the fundamental limitation that the constitutional scheme imposes on the Commerce Clause to protect the `States as States' is one of process, rather than one of result. Any substantive restraint on the exercise of Commerce Clause powers must find its justification in the procedural nature of this basic limitation, and it must be tailored to compensate for possible failings in the national political process, rather than to dictate a `sacred province of state autonomy.'\,''
+\end{quote}
+\noindent --- Blackmun, J., for the Court, \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/469/528/}{469 U.S. at 554}.
+
+\begin{quote}
+``The States' role in our system of government is a matter of constitutional law, not of legislative grace.''
+\end{quote}
+\noindent --- Powell, J., dissenting, 469 U.S.\ at 567.
+
+\begin{quote}
+``The Court today surveys the battle scene of federalism and sounds a retreat.''
+\end{quote}
+\noindent --- O'Connor, J., dissenting, 469 U.S.\ at 580.
+
+\begin{quote}
+``I do not think it incumbent on those of us in dissent to spell out further the fine points of a principle that will, I am confident, in time again command the support of a majority of this Court.''
+\end{quote}
+\noindent --- Rehnquist, J., dissenting, 469 U.S.\ at 580 (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/469/528/}{Justia}).
 
-\paragraph{Hypothetical 2 (``Opposite Side'' Contrast): Federal Shift Caps for Police and Firefighters.}
-Suppose Congress caps maximum shift lengths for municipal police and firefighters. \textbf{Under \textit{Garcia}:} If framed as a generally applicable safety rule covering both public and private emergency/security workers, it is valid; States must seek statutory relief through Congress (though if it targets public agencies exclusively, States may invoke the exclusivity question reserved in \textit{Reno}, 528 U.S. at 151). \textbf{Opposite Side (Under \textit{National League of Cities} / Powell \& O'Connor Dissents):} The law would be struck down regardless of general applicability, because police and fire services were paradigmatic ``traditional governmental functions'' (426 U.S. at 851), and dictating shift structures directly displaces core local fiscal and operational choices.
 
