-\end{description}
-
-\section{Facts and Procedural History}
+\section{Memory Jogger}
+Private racially restrictive covenants do not themselves violate the Fourteenth Amendment, but a state court's enforcement of them is state action that denies equal protection; a unanimous six-Justice Court reversed Missouri and Michigan decrees ousting Black families from homes they had bought from willing sellers (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/334/1/}{Justia}).
 
+\section{Detailed Case Facts}
 \textit{Shelley v. Kraemer} consolidated two state-court appeals---one from Missouri and one from Michigan---testing whether state courts could enforce private racially restrictive covenants barring non-white families from owning or occupying residential real estate (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/334/1/}{334 U.S. at 4--7}).
 
