-\section{Doctrinal Significance and Modern Legacy (Through 2026)}
-
-\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/419/345/}{\emph{Jackson}} marked the Burger Court's decisive retrenchment of Warren-era state-action doctrine, anchoring modern litigation around two durable constraints: (1) a \textbf{close-nexus requirement} tying state involvement to the specific challenged act, and (2) an \textbf{exclusivity requirement} limiting the public-function doctrine to powers ``traditionally exclusively reserved to the State.''
-
-\subsection{Consolidation in the Burger and Rehnquist Courts (1978--2001)}
-The Court extended \emph{Jackson}'s exclusivity and compulsion limits across private economic and social-welfare disputes in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/436/149/}{\emph{Flagg Bros., Inc. v. Brooks}}, 436 U.S. 149 (1978) (warehouseman's UCC lien sale), \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/457/991/}{\emph{Blum v. Yaretsky}}, 457 U.S. 991 (1982) (Medicaid-funded nursing homes), \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/457/830/}{\emph{Rendell-Baker v. Kohn}}, 457 U.S. 830 (1982) (publicly funded private school), \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/457/922}{\emph{Lugar v. Edmondson Oil Co.}}, 457 U.S. 922 (1982) (two-part fair-attribution test), and \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/526/40}{\emph{American Manufacturers Mutual Insurance Co. v. Sullivan}}, 526 U.S. 40 (1999) (workers'-compensation utilization review). Narrow exceptions survived for outsourced constitutional duties (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/487/42/}{\emph{West v. Atkins}}, 487 U.S. 42 (1988)), courtroom jury strikes (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/500/614/}{\emph{Edmonson v. Leesville Concrete Co.}}, 500 U.S. 614 (1991)), and ``pervasive entwinement'' (\href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/531/288}{\emph{Brentwood Academy v. TSSAA}}, 531 U.S. 288, 295 (2001)).
-
-\subsection{Modern Digital, Media, and Charter-School Legacy (2019--2026)}
-\begin{itemize}
-  \item \textbf{\href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/17-1702}{\emph{Manhattan Community Access Corp. v. Halleck}}, 587 U.S. 802 (2019):} Relying directly on \emph{Jackson} (419 U.S. at 350, 352--354), the Court held 5--4 that operating public-access cable channels is not a traditional, exclusive public function, and that municipal designation, heavy regulation, and rights-of-way access do not make a private speech forum a state actor.
-  \item \textbf{\href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-611}{\emph{Lindke v. Freed}}, 601 U.S. 187 (2024)} (and \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/601/22-324/}{\emph{O'Connor-Ratcliff v. Garnier}}, 601 U.S.\ \_\_\_ (2024)): Applying fair-attribution principles descending from \emph{Jackson}, \emph{Lugar}, and \emph{Halleck}, a unanimous Court held that a public official's social-media blocking is state action only if the official (1) had actual authority to speak for the State on that matter and (2) purported to exercise it in the relevant posts. In \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/603/23-411/}{\emph{Murthy v. Missouri}}, 603 U.S.\ \_\_\_ (2024), the Court dismissed federal social-media ``jawboning'' claims on Article~III standing without resolving coercion merits.
-  \item \textbf{\href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supct/cert/24-394}{\emph{Oklahoma Statewide Charter School Board v. Drummond}}, 605 U.S. 165 (2025):} Petitioners invoked \emph{Jackson}, \emph{Rendell-Baker}, and \emph{Halleck} to argue that a publicly funded religious charter school is not a state actor. On May 22, 2025, an equally divided Court (4--4, Barrett, J., recused) affirmed the Oklahoma Supreme Court's invalidation without opinion, leaving charter-school state action unresolved nationwide through 2026.
-\end{itemize}
-
-\section{Pedagogical Critiques and Exam Hypotheticals}
-
-\subsection{The ``Opposite Side'' Contrast: \emph{Jackson} vs.\ \emph{Memphis Light, Gas \& Water Div. v. Craft} (1978)}
-Four years after \emph{Jackson}, \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/436/1/}{\emph{Memphis Light, Gas \& Water Div. v. Craft}}, 436 U.S. 1 (1978), reached the opposite result on nearly identical utility-shutoff facts solely because the utility was \textbf{municipally owned}. With state action automatic, the Court held that Tennessee's ``for cause'' rule created a protected property interest requiring pre-termination notice and an opportunity to contest billing errors before designated personnel. The pairing highlights \emph{Jackson}'s formalist cliff: identical shutoffs by sole-provider monopolies trigger Fourteenth Amendment due process or none at all depending entirely on municipal versus private corporate ownership.
-
-\subsection{Core Critiques}
-Critics emphasize three tensions: (1) \textbf{seriatim disaggregation} (Douglas, J.), dismissing regulation, monopoly, and tariff approval individually rather than cumulatively; (2) the \textbf{legal-realist baseline problem}, treating utility self-help and PUC silence as ``neutral'' private action despite positive state-law backing; and (3) \textbf{weak pluralism justification} (Marshall, J.), shielding an investor-owned monopoly rather than individual associational liberty.
-
-\subsection{Exam Hypotheticals}
-\begin{enumerate}
-  \item \textbf{PUC-Mandated Shutoff:} A state utility commission issues an emergency order directing private electric utilities to remotely disconnect high-usage smart meters without a hearing. \emph{Analysis:} Unlike \emph{Jackson}'s passive tariff filing, state compulsion establishes a close nexus (419 U.S. at 357; \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/457/991/}{\emph{Blum}}), triggering \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/436/1/}{\emph{Craft}} due-process scrutiny.
-  \item \textbf{Dominant Platform vs.\ Mayor's Page:} A private social-media monopoly using public fiber rights-of-way bans a critic after a governor's aide emails calling the posts ``misinformation''; separately, a mayor blocks the critic from an unmarked personal page used for official boil-water orders. \emph{Analysis:} The platform is not a state actor under \emph{Jackson}/\href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/17-1702}{\emph{Halleck}} absent coercive threats (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/603/23-411/}{\emph{Murthy}}), whereas the mayor's page-wide block likely satisfies \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-611}{\emph{Lindke}}'s two-prong test.
-  \item \textbf{Charter School vs.\ Prison Clinic:} A 100\% state-funded private charter school expels a student without a hearing; a private prison healthcare contractor withholds insulin. \emph{Analysis:} The prison contractor is a state actor under \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/487/42/}{\emph{West v. Atkins}}; the charter school sits on the unresolved \emph{Jackson}/\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/457/830/}{\emph{Rendell-Baker}}/\href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supct/cert/24-394}{\emph{Drummond}} boundary between non-exclusive education and statutory public-entity entwinement (\href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/531/288}{\emph{Brentwood}}).
-\end{enumerate}
 
