+\textbf{Holding:} Yes. In a 5--4 decision authored by Chief Justice Roberts, the Supreme Court held that \textbf{\S~4(b) of the Voting Rights Act is unconstitutional} because its coverage formula is based on 40-year-old data and eradicated practices that no longer speak to current conditions, and therefore can no longer be used as a basis for subjecting jurisdictions to federal preclearance under \S~5 (\href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/12-96}{Cornell LII, Roberts Op. Part III-D}).
 
 The Court expressly declined to hold \S~5 itself unconstitutional and emphasized that its ruling ``in no way affects the permanent, nationwide ban on racial discrimination in voting found in \S~2.'' Practically, however, invalidating \S~4(b) rendered \S~5's preclearance regime inoperable nationwide unless and until Congress enacts a new coverage formula grounded in current conditions (aside from targeted judicial ``bail-in'' orders under \S~3(c), 52 U.S.C. \S~10302(c)).
 
