-\end{enumerate}
+\item \textbf{Proving Intent (\textit{Arlington Heights}):} The following Term, in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/429/252/}{\emph{Village of Arlington Heights v. Metropolitan Housing Development Corp.}}, 429 U.S. 252 (1977), the Court codified the multi-factor evidentiary framework for proving discriminatory intent behind facially neutral official action: (1) stark disparate impact (noting that cases as clear as \emph{Yick Wo} and \emph{Gomillion} are ``rare''); (2) historical background; (3) the specific sequence of events leading to the decision; (4) procedural or substantive departures from normal practice; and (5) contemporary legislative or administrative statements.
 
-\section{Doctrinal Significance and Modern Legacy (Through 2026)}
+\item \textbf{Selective Prosecution (\textit{Armstrong}):} Nearly a century later, in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/517/456/}{\emph{United States v. Armstrong}}, 517 U.S. 456 (1996), the Court cited \emph{Yick Wo} while imposing a demanding threshold even to obtain \emph{discovery} on a selective-prosecution claim: a criminal defendant must produce ``some evidence'' of both discriminatory effect (that similarly situated individuals of a different race were \emph{not} prosecuted) and discriminatory intent, overcoming a strong presumption of prosecutorial regularity.
 
-\emph{Yick Wo v. Hopkins} stands as one of the foundational pillars of modern Equal Protection doctrine, though its subsequent trajectory reveals how sharply the Supreme Court has policed the boundary between \emph{discriminatory purpose} and mere \emph{disparate impact}.
+\end{itemize}
 
-\subsection{The Discriminatory-Purpose Requirement: \emph{Washington v. Davis}, \emph{Arlington Heights}, and \emph{Feeney}}
-During the twentieth century, the Court invoked \emph{Yick Wo} alongside \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/364/339/}{\emph{Gomillion v. Lightfoot}}, 364 U.S. 339 (1960) (invalidating an uncouth 28-sided municipal boundary redrawing that excluded almost all Black voters from Tuskegee, Alabama), as the archetype of a facially neutral law whose operation is ``unexplainable on grounds other than race.'' In \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/426/229/}{\emph{Washington v. Davis}}, 426 U.S. 229 (1976), however, the Court squarely rejected a constitutional disparate-impact theory: holding that a facially neutral police entrance exam (Test 21) did not violate equal protection merely because Black applicants failed at four times the rate of white applicants, the Court ruled that challengers must prove a \emph{racially discriminatory purpose}. \emph{Davis} reframed \emph{Yick Wo} not as a disparate-impact precedent, but as an extreme case in which the statistical disparity was so stark and unexplained that discriminatory intent was inescapable.
+\section{Examples: Future Applications}
+\begin{enumerate}
 
-The following Term, in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/429/252/}{\emph{Village of Arlington Heights v. Metropolitan Housing Development Corp.}}, 429 U.S. 252 (1977), the Court codified the multi-factor evidentiary framework for proving discriminatory intent behind facially neutral official action: (1) stark disparate impact (noting that cases as clear as \emph{Yick Wo} and \emph{Gomillion} are ``rare''); (2) historical background; (3) the specific sequence of events leading to the decision; (4) procedural or substantive departures from normal practice; and (5) contemporary legislative or administrative statements. Two years later, \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/442/256/}{\emph{Personnel Administrator of Massachusetts v. Feeney}}, 442 U.S. 256 (1979), tightened the intent requirement further, holding that ``discriminatory purpose'' requires showing that decisionmakers acted ``because of,'' not merely ``in spite of,'' an adverse effect on an identifiable group.
+\item \textbf{Same-Side (Discriminatory Administration): Pacifica's ``Fire Resilience Permits.''} The City of Pacifica requires all street-food commissaries in pre-1970 buildings to obtain a ``Fire Resilience Permit'' from a Licensing Board empowered to grant or deny applications ``consistent with neighborhood safety,'' with no objective standards. Ninety percent of pre-1970 commissaries are operated by Central American immigrants (many noncitizens). Over two years, the Board denies 142 of 145 applications from Central American operators---including 110 with clean fire-marshal inspections---while granting 38 of 40 applications from non-Hispanic operators in comparable buildings, citing only ``neighborhood congestion.'' Under \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/118/356/}{\emph{Yick Wo}} (118 U.S. at 369, 373--374), noncitizens are ``persons'' protected by the Equal Protection Clause. The extreme disparity (97.9\% vs.\ 5\% denial rates), clean safety inspections, and standardless discretion establish administration with ``an evil eye and an unequal hand,'' supporting an inference of purposeful national-origin or alienage discrimination under \emph{Davis} and \emph{Arlington Heights} that fails strict scrutiny.
 
-\subsection{Selective Prosecution and Enforcement: From \emph{Ah Sin} to \emph{Armstrong} and \emph{Gonzalez v. Trevino}}
-Because \emph{Yick Wo} invalidated discriminatory administration of a criminal ordinance, it became the doctrinal fountainhead for selective-prosecution and selective-enforcement claims. Yet the Court quickly erected steep evidentiary hurdles. In \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/198/500/}{\emph{Ah Sin v. Wittman}}, 198 U.S. 500 (1905), the Court rejected a Chinese defendant's \emph{Yick Wo} challenge to a San Francisco anti-gambling ordinance because he failed to prove that non-Chinese violators existed and were not prosecuted. Nearly a century later, in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/517/456/}{\emph{United States v. Armstrong}}, 517 U.S. 456 (1996), the Court cited \emph{Yick Wo} while imposing a demanding threshold even to obtain \emph{discovery} on a selective-prosecution claim: a criminal defendant must produce ``some evidence'' of both discriminatory effect (that similarly situated individuals of a different race were \emph{not} prosecuted) and discriminatory intent, overcoming a strong presumption of prosecutorial regularity.
+\item \textbf{Same-Side (Selective Enforcement of a Neutral Code): Sidewalk-Vending Citations.} A city ordinance bars sidewalk vending within 50 feet of a transit stop. Police records show that over three years officers issued 212 citations under the ordinance, 205 of them to vendors of Somali origin, although city inspectors' own photo surveys document dozens of other vendors at the same stops on the same days who were never cited. The police department offers no explanation for the pattern. As in \textit{Yick Wo}, the law is neutral on its face, but its administration draws a line between similarly situated persons by national origin, and the comparator evidence is concrete and unrebutted. That record is the kind of stark, unexplained pattern from which \textit{Davis} and \textit{Arlington Heights} allow a court to infer purposeful discrimination, so the citations violate equal protection.
 
-In the First Amendment retaliatory-arrest context, the Court borrowed directly from \emph{Armstrong} and \emph{Yick Wo}'s comparator logic in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/587/17-1174/}{\emph{Nieves v. Bartlett}}, 587 U.S. 391 (2019), creating an exception to the no-probable-cause bar when a plaintiff presents objective evidence that similarly situated persons not engaged in protected speech were not arrested. In \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/602/22-1025/}{\emph{Gonzalez v. Trevino}}, 602 U.S. 653 (2024), the Court clarified that a plaintiff need not produce ``virtually identical'' comparator arrests to satisfy \emph{Nieves}; statistical evidence showing a statute had never been used to arrest anyone for similar conduct over a decade could suffice---an approach echoing \emph{Yick Wo}'s willingness to infer improper administration from an anomalous enforcement record.
+\item \textbf{Opposite-Side (Disparate Impact Without Purpose): Pacifica's Uniform Safety Code.} Pacifica instead repeals the permit system and requires \emph{every} commercial commissary citywide to install a UL-listed \$45,000 fire-suppression system, with no waivers and purely mechanical inspection. Because immigrant operators disproportionately lease smaller, older buildings, 85\% of Central American-owned commissaries close versus 12\% of corporate-backed commissaries. Council minutes show actuaries recommended the exact specification after three fatal kitchen fires, with no evidence of racial or anti-immigrant animus. This regulation tracks \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/113/27/}{\emph{Barbier v.\ Connolly}}, 113 U.S. 27 (1885), and \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/113/703/}{\emph{Soon Hing v.\ Crowley}}, 113 U.S. 703 (1885), rather than \emph{Yick Wo}. Under \emph{Davis} and \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/442/256/}{\emph{Personnel Administrator of Massachusetts v.\ Feeney}}, 442 U.S. 256, 279 (1979), foreseeable disparate impact alone does not trigger strict scrutiny unless Pacifica acted ``because of,'' not merely ``in spite of,'' its effect on immigrants. Supported by an objective fire-safety record and uniform enforcement, the code survives rational basis review.
 
-\subsection{Equal Protection Rights of Noncitizens}
-\emph{Yick Wo}'s holding that ``any person'' includes noncitizens launched an enduring line of alienage jurisprudence, including \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/239/33/}{\emph{Truax v. Raich}}, 239 U.S. 33 (1915) (striking down an Arizona quota restricting alien employment), \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/403/365/}{\emph{Graham v. Richardson}}, 403 U.S. 365 (1971) (applying strict scrutiny to state alienage classifications denying welfare benefits), and \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/457/202/}{\emph{Plyler v. Doe}}, 457 U.S. 202 (1982) (confirming that even undocumented children are ``persons within [a State's] jurisdiction'' protected against denial of public K--12 education). At the same time, the Court carved out major exceptions: federal alienage classifications receive deferential review under Congress's plenary immigration power (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/426/67/}{\emph{Mathews v. Diaz}}, 426 U.S. 67 (1976)), and states may restrict certain ``political function'' offices (such as police officers and public school teachers) to citizens (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/441/68/}{\emph{Ambach v. Norwick}}, 441 U.S. 68 (1979)).
+\item \textbf{Opposite-Side (No Comparator Proof): A Gambling Prosecution.} A defendant of Chinese descent is prosecuted under a facially neutral ordinance barring gambling rooms. He moves to dismiss on the ground that the ordinance is enforced only against Chinese-owned establishments, but he offers only general assertions and newspaper accounts of anti-Chinese sentiment; he identifies no non-Chinese gambling operators whom the police knew of and declined to charge. \textit{Yick Wo} does not help him. Its inference of hostility rested on an admitted record showing that similarly situated non-Chinese operators were treated differently. Without evidence that comparable violators of another race or nationality went unprosecuted, the claim fails, as it did in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/198/500/}{\emph{Ah Sin v. Wittman}}, 198 U.S. 500 (1905), and under \textit{Armstrong} he would not even obtain discovery.
 
-\subsection{Contemporary Equal Protection Doctrine Through 2026}
-Through 2026, \emph{Yick Wo} continues to anchor both anti-classification rhetoric and high-stakes litigation over facially neutral policies:
-\begin{itemize}
-    \item \textbf{Universal Anti-Discrimination Principle:} In \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/600/20-1199/}{\emph{Students for Fair Admissions, Inc. v. President and Fellows of Harvard College}}, 600 U.S. 181 (2023), Chief Justice Roberts cited \emph{Yick Wo} early in the opinion to establish that the Fourteenth Amendment's ``broad and benign provisions'' apply universally ``to all persons'' and condemn all unjustified ``hostility to . . . race and nationality''---framing the Clause's core command as colorblind equality.
-    \item \textbf{Proof of Intent in Executive, Redistricting, and Legislative Challenges:} Modern challengers invoking \emph{Yick Wo} and \emph{Arlington Heights} face formidable evidentiary burdens. In \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/591/18-587/}{\emph{Department of Homeland Security v. Regents of the University of California}}, 591 U.S. 1 (2020), a plurality rejected an Equal Protection challenge to the rescission of DACA despite its overwhelming impact on Latino recipients, finding the allegations insufficient under \emph{Arlington Heights}. Similarly, in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/602/22-807/}{\emph{Alexander v. South Carolina State Conference of the NAACP}}, 602 U.S. 1 (2024), the Court reinforced a strong ``presumption of legislative good faith'' in racial-gerrymandering challenges, requiring plaintiffs to disentangle race from partisan politics and faulting them for not producing an alternative map. And in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/605/23-477/}{\emph{United States v. Skrmetti}}, 605 U.S. \_\_\_ (2025), the Court held that Tennessee's restriction on certain medical treatments for minors classified on the basis of age and medical use rather than sex or transgender status, underscoring how decisively the threshold classification-versus-intent inquiry controls modern Equal Protection outcomes.
-\end{itemize}
+\item \textbf{Fence-Sitter (Standardless Discretion, Moderate Disparity): Food-Truck Permits.} A city's permit board may grant or deny food-truck permits in the public interest, with no written criteria. Over two years it denies 45 percent of applications from noncitizen operators and 20 percent from citizen operators. The board points to some neutral reasons in its files (incomplete applications, parking conflicts), but the reasons are applied inconsistently, and one board member once remarked that the city had too many immigrant vendors. The standardless grant of power echoes the ordinances in \textit{Yick Wo}, and the remark and irregular reasons are \textit{Arlington Heights} evidence. But the disparity is far from \textit{Yick Wo}'s 200-to-0 record, the board has offered partial neutral explanations, and later cases such as \textit{Kotch} largely abandoned \textit{Yick Wo}'s arbitrary-power strand for economic regulation. Whether a court infers purposeful discrimination is genuinely uncertain.
 
-\section{Pedagogical Critiques and Exam Hypotheticals}
+\end{enumerate}
 
-\subsection{Pedagogical Critiques and Doctrinal Tensions}
-\begin{itemize}
-    \item \textbf{Facial Arbitrariness vs.\ As-Applied Animus:} Justice Matthews blended two distinct theories: a structural objection to ``naked and arbitrary power'' over a lawful occupation and an Equal Protection objection to racially discriminatory enforcement. Later decisions largely abandoned the first strand for ordinary economic regulation (compare \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/330/552/}{\emph{Kotch v.\ Board of River Port Pilot Commissioners}}, 330 U.S. 552 (1947)) while canonizing the second for suspect classifications.
-    \item \textbf{An Evidentiary Unicorn:} Because San Francisco stipulated to its 200-to-0 versus 80-to-1 permitting record and offered no safety rationale, \emph{Yick Wo} is an outlier. By treating \emph{Yick Wo} as the benchmark for inferring discriminatory purpose from disparate impact (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/426/229/}{\emph{Washington v.\ Davis}}, 426 U.S. 229 (1976); \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/429/252/}{\emph{Arlington Heights}}, 429 U.S. 252 (1977); \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/517/456/}{\emph{United States v.\ Armstrong}}, 517 U.S. 456 (1996)), modern doctrine makes subtler or mixed-motive discrimination far harder to prove.
-    \item \textbf{Exclusion-Era Irony:} Decided four years after the Chinese Exclusion Act of 1882, \emph{Yick Wo} robustly protected resident noncitizens' livelihoods against local expropriation even as the Court deferred broadly to federal exclusion and deportation powers.
-\end{itemize}
+\section{Critique}
+\textbf{Facial Arbitrariness vs.\ As-Applied Animus:} Justice Matthews blended two distinct theories: a structural objection to ``naked and arbitrary power'' over a lawful occupation and an Equal Protection objection to racially discriminatory enforcement. Later decisions largely abandoned the first strand for ordinary economic regulation (compare \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/330/552/}{\emph{Kotch v.\ Board of River Port Pilot Commissioners}}, 330 U.S. 552 (1947)) while canonizing the second for suspect classifications.
 
-\subsection{Exam Hypothetical 1: Discretionary Permits and Stark Disparity}
-\textbf{Fact Pattern:} The City of Pacifica requires all street-food commissaries in pre-1970 buildings to obtain a ``Fire Resilience Permit'' from a Licensing Board empowered to grant or deny applications ``consistent with neighborhood safety,'' with no objective standards. Ninety percent of pre-1970 commissaries are operated by Central American immigrants (many noncitizens). Over two years, the Board denies 142 of 145 applications from Central American operators---including 110 with clean fire-marshal inspections---while granting 38 of 40 applications from non-Hispanic operators in comparable buildings, citing only ``neighborhood congestion.''
+\textbf{An Evidentiary Unicorn:} Because San Francisco stipulated to its 200-to-0 versus 80-to-1 permitting record and offered no safety rationale, \emph{Yick Wo} is an outlier. By treating \emph{Yick Wo} as the benchmark for inferring discriminatory purpose from disparate impact (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/426/229/}{\emph{Washington v.\ Davis}}, 426 U.S. 229 (1976); \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/429/252/}{\emph{Arlington Heights}}, 429 U.S. 252 (1977); \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/517/456/}{\emph{United States v.\ Armstrong}}, 517 U.S. 456 (1996)), modern doctrine makes subtler or mixed-motive discrimination far harder to prove.
 
-\textbf{Analysis:} Under \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/118/356/}{\emph{Yick Wo}} (118 U.S. at 369, 373--374), noncitizens are ``persons'' protected by the Equal Protection Clause. The extreme disparity (97.9\% vs.\ 5\% denial rates), clean safety inspections, and standardless discretion establish administration with ``an evil eye and an unequal hand,'' supporting an inference of purposeful national-origin or alienage discrimination under \emph{Davis} and \emph{Arlington Heights} that fails strict scrutiny.
+\textbf{Exclusion-Era Irony:} Decided four years after the Chinese Exclusion Act of 1882, \emph{Yick Wo} robustly protected resident noncitizens' livelihoods against local expropriation even as the Court deferred broadly to federal exclusion and deportation powers.
 
-\subsection{Exam Hypothetical 2 (``Opposite Side'' Contrast): Uniform Objective Safety Code}
-\textbf{Fact Pattern:} Pacifica instead repeals the permit system and requires \emph{every} commercial commissary citywide to install a UL-listed \$45,000 fire-suppression system, with no waivers and purely mechanical inspection. Because immigrant operators disproportionately lease smaller, older buildings, 85\% of Central American-owned commissaries close versus 12\% of corporate-backed commissaries. Council minutes show actuaries recommended the exact specification after three fatal kitchen fires, with no evidence of racial or anti-immigrant animus.
+\section{Key Quotations}
+\begin{quote}
