-\section{Concurring and Dissenting Opinions}
+\subsection{Concurring Opinion (Justice Marshall)}
+
+Justice Marshall joined the majority opinion while reiterating his dissent in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/411/1/}{\textit{San Antonio Independent School District v.\ Rodriguez}}, 411 U.S. 1, 70--133 (1973). He maintained that education is a fundamental interest and that \textit{Plyler} vindicated his flexible ``sliding-scale'' approach to Equal Protection, calibrating scrutiny according to both the societal importance of the interest affected and the invidiousness of the classification.
+
+\subsection{Concurring Opinion (Justice Blackmun)}
+
+Having joined the majority in \textit{Rodriguez}, Justice Blackmun wrote separately to reconcile the two cases. He analogized the \textit{complete denial} of basic education to the denial of the right to vote: though neither is explicitly enumerated in the Constitution, denying either relegates an identifiable group to permanent second-class status, striking at the heart of Equal Protection. Unlike the funding disparities in \textit{Rodriguez}, \S~21.031 effected an absolute deprivation. He also stressed federal preemption concerns: because states cannot determine which undocumented children will ultimately be permitted to remain, \S~21.031 was fatally imprecise.
+
+\subsection{Concurring Opinion (Justice Powell)}
+
+Author of the \textit{Rodriguez} majority opinion, Justice Powell emphasized the ``unique character'' of \textit{Plyler}. Analogizing undocumented children to illegitimate children under \textit{Weber}, \textit{Trimble}, and \textit{Lalli v.\ Lalli}, 439 U.S. 259 (1978), he endorsed heightened intermediate scrutiny requiring that the state's means bear a ``fair and substantial relation'' to substantial state interests. He distinguished \textit{Rodriguez} because \S~21.031 singled out a discrete class of innocent children for total exclusion based on parental misconduct, while expressing sympathy for border states burdened by Congress's lack of ``effective leadership'' on immigration.
+
+\subsection{Dissenting Opinion (Chief Justice Burger)}
+
+Chief Justice Burger agreed that undocumented aliens are ``within the jurisdiction'' of Texas and candidly remarked that, as a matter of social policy, depriving children of an elementary education is ``senseless'' and ``folly.'' Nevertheless, he accused the majority of acting as ``Platonic Guardians'' and engaging in unabashedly result-oriented policymaking to compensate for congressional inaction. Once the majority conceded that undocumented aliens are not a suspect class and education is not a fundamental right, settled doctrine mandated ordinary rational basis review. Under that deferential standard, Texas had a legitimate fiscal interest in conserving finite revenues for lawful residents, reinforced by federal exclusions of undocumented aliens from welfare programs (such as Food Stamps, AFDC, and Medicare). He criticized the majority's ``quasi-suspect-class and quasi-fundamental-rights'' patchwork as unprincipled, warned that \textit{Plyler} could not logically distinguish education from food, shelter, or health care, and predicted that the ruling's ``unique confluence of theories and rationales'' would ``likely stand for little beyond the results in these particular cases.''
+
