-``Lest any confusion on the point remain, we reaffirm today that a regulation of the time, place, or manner of protected speech must be narrowly tailored to serve the government's legitimate, content-neutral interests, but that it need not be the least restrictive or least intrusive means of doing so.'' \\
---- Justice Kennedy, for the Court, \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/491/781/}{491 U.S. at 798--799}.
+``Lest any confusion on the point remain, we reaffirm today that a regulation of the time, place, or manner of protected speech must be narrowly tailored to serve the government's legitimate, content-neutral interests, but that it need not be the least restrictive or least intrusive means of doing so.''
 \end{quote}
+\noindent --- Justice Kennedy, for the Court, \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/491/781/}{491 U.S. at 798--799}.
 
 \begin{quote}
-``Under more familiar prior restraints, government officials censor speech `by a simple stroke of the pen' \dots\ Here, it is done by a single turn of a knob. \dots\ Today, the majority enshrines efficacy, but sacrifices free speech.'' \\
---- Justice Marshall, dissenting, \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/491/781/}{491 U.S. at 807--808}.
+``Under more familiar prior restraints, government officials censor speech `by a simple stroke of the pen' \dots\ Here, it is done by a single turn of a knob. \dots\ Today, the majority enshrines efficacy, but sacrifices free speech.''
