-\section{Facts and Procedural History}
+\begin{center}
+{\LARGE \textit{R.A.V. v. City of St. Paul}, 505 U.S. 377 (1992)\par}
+\vspace{0.8em}
+{\large Case Brief\par}
+\end{center}
+\vspace{1em}
+
+\section{Memory Jogger}
+After a teenager burned a cross in a Black family's yard, the Court unanimously struck down St.~Paul's bias-motivated crime ordinance; Justice Scalia's five-Justice majority held that even within an unprotected category like fighting words, government may not single out disfavored topics or viewpoints for punishment (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/505/377/}{Justia}).
+
+\section{Detailed Case Facts}
 In the predawn hours of June~21, 1990, petitioner R.A.V. (then a juvenile) and several other teenagers allegedly taped together broken chair legs to form a crude cross and burned it inside the fenced yard of a Black family living across the street from the home where R.A.V. was staying in St. Paul, Minnesota. Although Minnesota law provided several content-neutral criminal charges for the conduct---including terroristic threats, arson, and criminal damage to property---the City of St. Paul charged R.A.V. under its Bias-Motivated Crime Ordinance, St. Paul, Minn., Legis. Code \S~292.02 (1990). That ordinance made it a misdemeanor to place on public or private property ``a symbol, object, appellation, characterization or graffiti, including, but not limited to, a burning cross or Nazi swastika, which one knows or has reasonable grounds to know arouses anger, alarm or resentment in others on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or gender.''
 
+\section{Procedural History}
 R.A.V. moved to dismiss the charge, arguing that the ordinance was substantially overbroad and impermissibly content-based in violation of the First Amendment. The trial court granted the motion and dismissed the count. The Minnesota Supreme Court reversed, 464~N.W.2d~507 (Minn.~1991), rejecting the overbreadth challenge by construing the phrase ``arouses anger, alarm or resentment in others'' to reach only ``fighting words'' under \textit{Chaplinsky v. New Hampshire}, 315~U.S.~568, 572 (1942)---expression unprotected by the First Amendment. It further held that any content-based distinction survived strict scrutiny because the ordinance was narrowly tailored to serve a compelling interest in protecting the community against bias-motivated threats to public safety and order. The U.S. Supreme Court granted certiorari.
 
-\section{Issue(s) Presented}
-Whether a municipal ordinance that prohibits only a content-defined subset of ``fighting words''---those that arouse anger, alarm, or resentment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion, or gender---is facially invalid under the First Amendment.
+\section{Judicial Votes}
+The Supreme Court \textbf{reversed and remanded} the Minnesota Supreme Court's judgment. The judgment was unanimous (9--0), but the Justices divided 5--4 on the rationale. The case was argued on December~4, 1991, and decided on June~22, 1992 (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/505/377/}{Justia}; \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/90-7675}{Cornell LII}):
+
+\begin{itemize}
+    \item \textbf{Majority Opinion:} Justice Scalia delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Rehnquist and Justices Kennedy, Souter, and Thomas.
+    \item \textbf{Concurring Opinions:} Justice White concurred in the judgment, joined by Justices Blackmun and O'Connor, and by Justice Stevens except as to Part~I-A. Justice Blackmun concurred in the judgment. Justice Stevens concurred in the judgment, joined by Justices White and Blackmun as to Part~I.
+    \item \textbf{Dissenting Opinions:} None.
+\end{itemize}
 
 \section{Holding}
-Yes. Even accepting the Minnesota Supreme Court's narrowing construction limiting the ordinance to proscribable ``fighting words,'' the St. Paul ordinance is facially unconstitutional because it engages in impermissible content and viewpoint discrimination within a proscribable category of speech and is not necessary to serve a compelling governmental interest.
+\textbf{Questions Presented:}
+
+Whether a municipal ordinance that prohibits only a content-defined subset of ``fighting words''---those that arouse anger, alarm, or resentment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion, or gender---is facially invalid under the First Amendment.
+
+\textbf{Holding:} Yes. Even accepting the Minnesota Supreme Court's narrowing construction limiting the ordinance to proscribable ``fighting words,'' the St. Paul ordinance is facially unconstitutional because it engages in impermissible content and viewpoint discrimination within a proscribable category of speech and is not necessary to serve a compelling governmental interest.
+
+\section{Analysis of Opinions}
+\subsection{Majority Opinion (Justice Scalia)}
 
-\section{Reasoning}
 Writing for a five-Justice majority, Justice Scalia accepted as binding the Minnesota Supreme Court's construction of the ordinance as reaching only \textit{Chaplinsky} fighting words, and bypassed overbreadth to invalidate the law for content and viewpoint discrimination:
 \begin{enumerate}
   \item \textbf{Proscribable Categories Are Not ``Invisible to the Constitution'':} Traditional categorical exceptions---obscenity, defamation, and fighting words---may be regulated because of their constitutionally proscribable content, but they are not ``entirely invisible to the Constitution.'' Like a noisy sound truck, fighting words are an unprotected \textit{mode} of communication, yet government may not regulate them based on hostility or favoritism toward the underlying nonproscribable message expressed (e.g., banning only libel critical of the government).
@@ -53,50 +57,65 @@ Writing for a five-Justice majority, Justice Scalia accepted as binding the Minn
   \item \textbf{Failure of Strict Scrutiny:} Although ensuring the basic human rights of historically discriminated-against groups is a compelling state interest, content discrimination was not ``reasonably necessary'' to achieve it; a content-neutral ban on \textit{all} fighting words would have produced the exact same protective effect without licensing official hostility toward particular viewpoints.
 \end{enumerate}
 
-\section{Concurring and Dissenting Opinions}
+\subsection{Concurring in the Judgment (Justice White)}
+
+Justice White argued that the Court should have invalidated the ordinance on settled \textit{overbreadth} grounds because the Minnesota Supreme Court's construction still criminalized protected expression that merely causes ``anger, alarm or resentment.'' White sharply attacked Scalia's new ``underbreadth'' theory as abandoning the categorical approach, collapsing strict scrutiny, and inventing ad hoc exceptions that either swallowed the rule or jeopardized Title~VII hostile-workplace claims.
+
+\subsection{Concurring in the Judgment (Justice Blackmun)}
+
+Justice Blackmun joined White's overbreadth analysis and warned that the majority either weakened First Amendment scrutiny across the board by dismantling categorical distinctions or issued an unprincipled aberration driven by opposition to ``politically correct speech.''
+
+\subsection{Concurring in the Judgment (Justice Stevens)}
+
+Justice Stevens rejected both Scalia's near-absolute anti-content-discrimination rule and White's rigid categorical approach (Part~I-A). Favoring a contextual, multi-factor inquiry weighing content, context, and scope, Stevens argued that St. Paul's ordinance evenhandedly barred ``punches below the belt'' based on qualitatively greater social harms and would have been constitutional had it not been overbroad.
+
+\subsection{Subsequent Developments}
+
 \begin{itemize}
-  \item \textbf{Justice White, concurring in the judgment (joined by Justices Blackmun and O'Connor, and by Justice Stevens except Part~I-A):} Argued that the Court should have invalidated the ordinance on settled \textit{overbreadth} grounds because the Minnesota Supreme Court's construction still criminalized protected expression that merely causes ``anger, alarm or resentment.'' White sharply attacked Scalia's new ``underbreadth'' theory as abandoning the categorical approach, collapsing strict scrutiny, and inventing ad hoc exceptions that either swallowed the rule or jeopardized Title~VII hostile-workplace claims.
-  \item \textbf{Justice Blackmun, concurring in the judgment:} Joined White's overbreadth analysis and warned that the majority either weakened First Amendment scrutiny across the board by dismantling categorical distinctions or issued an unprincipled aberration driven by opposition to ``politically correct speech.''
-  \item \textbf{Justice Stevens, concurring in the judgment (joined by Justices White and Blackmun in Part~I):} Rejected both Scalia's near-absolute anti-content-discrimination rule and White's rigid categorical approach (Part~I-A). Favoring a contextual, multi-factor inquiry weighing content, context, and scope, Stevens argued that St. Paul's ordinance evenhandedly barred ``punches below the belt'' based on qualitatively greater social harms and would have been constitutional had it not been overbroad.
+
+\item \textbf{Hate-Crime Penalty Enhancements (\textit{Wisconsin v. Mitchell}):} Just one year later, a unanimous Court in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/508/476/}{\textit{Wisconsin v. Mitchell}, 508~U.S.~476 (1993)}, upheld a statute enhancing sentences for aggravated battery when the defendant intentionally selected the victim based on race. Chief Justice Rehnquist distinguished \textit{R.A.V.} on the ground that \textit{Mitchell} regulated bias-motivated \textit{conduct} (violent crime) rather than protected or proscribable \textit{expression}.
+  \item \textbf{Applying \textit{R.A.V.}'s First Exception (\textit{Virginia v. Black}):} In \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/538/343/}{\textit{Virginia v. Black}, 538~U.S.~343 (2003)}, the Court held that a state may ban cross burning carried out ``with an intent to intimidate'' without violating \textit{R.A.V.}, because cross burning is a particularly virulent form of ``true threat'' fitting \textit{R.A.V.}'s first exception (though a plurality struck down Virginia's provision treating cross burning as prima facie evidence of intent to intimidate).
+
 \end{itemize}
 
+\section{Examples: Future Applications}
+\begin{enumerate}
+
+\item \textbf{Same-Side (Law Invalid): Fighting Words That Disparage the Police.} After tense street protests, a city makes it a crime to direct at a police officer any fighting words that disparage law enforcement, while leaving fighting words praising the police, or insulting the officer on other grounds, unregulated. A protester convicted for a face-to-face epithet challenges the ordinance. Under \textit{R.A.V.}, the city may ban fighting words because of their abusive mode, but it may not pick out only those that carry a disfavored message. This ordinance is worse than St.~Paul's: it discriminates by viewpoint on its face, licensing one side of the debate over policing to fight freestyle. The city's interest in protecting officers could be served by a neutral ban on all fighting words directed at anyone, so the law fails strict scrutiny.
+
+\item \textbf{Same-Side (Law Invalid): Obscenity Banned Only When It Mocks Officials.} A state already bars the sale of obscene material under the governing test for obscenity, then adds a felony enhancement that applies only to obscene videos that ridicule elected officials. A distributor of such a video challenges the enhancement. Obscenity is a proscribable category, but \textit{R.A.V.} holds that categories of unprotected speech are not ``entirely invisible to the Constitution.'' The enhancement does not target what makes obscenity unprotected, such as especially graphic prurient content; it targets political criticism, a message the State could never suppress directly. Like Justice Scalia's example of banning only libel critical of the government, the distinction suggests that official suppression of ideas is afoot, and none of the three exceptions saves it.
+
+\item \textbf{Opposite-Side (Law Likely Valid): Threats Against Election Workers.} A state law imposes heavier penalties for true threats of violence made against election officials and poll workers than for other true threats. A defendant convicted of threatening a county clerk argues that, under \textit{R.A.V.}, the law singles out threats on a particular subject. The State answers with \textit{R.A.V.}'s first exception. Justice Scalia explained that Congress may criminalize only threats against the President because the reasons threats are proscribable---fear, disruption, and the risk of violence---have special force there. The same reasoning applies to the officials who run elections: the law selects the subset of threats in which the very harms that make threats unprotected are most acute, and it does not favor any viewpoint on the election itself. The law is likely valid.
+
+\item \textbf{Opposite-Side (Prosecution Valid): Bias Enhancement for Conduct.} Suppose St.~Paul had charged R.A.V. under Minnesota's content-neutral laws against terroristic threats and criminal damage to property, and the State then sought a longer sentence under a statute that enhances penalties when the defendant intentionally selected the victim because of race. That prosecution would raise no \textit{R.A.V.} problem. The underlying crimes punish conduct without regard to any message, and the enhancement punishes the discriminatory selection of a victim, not the expression of an idea. As the Court later held in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/508/476/}{\textit{Wisconsin v. Mitchell}, 508~U.S.~476 (1993)}, bias-motivated conduct may be punished more severely, which is why Justice Scalia observed that St.~Paul had sufficient means to punish cross burning without the ordinance.
+
+\item \textbf{Fence-Sitter (Contested): A Campus Harassment Act.} Following a wave of campus confrontations, a state enacts the ``Campus Harassment Act,'' making it a misdemeanor to utter face-to-face fighting words on public university grounds ``that demean a person on the basis of nationality, religion, or political ideology.'' A student prosecuted for shouting a religious slur at a classmate challenges the statute on its face under the First Amendment. \textbf{Challenger:} Even if limited to \textit{Chaplinsky} fighting words, the statute selectively proscribes fighting words addressing three enumerated topics (nationality, religion, political ideology) while leaving unregulated equally provocative fighting words based on personal appearance, family, or athletics. Like St. Paul's ordinance, it fails strict scrutiny because a content-neutral ban on \textit{all} campus fighting words would equally protect campus order without singling out disfavored subjects. \textbf{State:} The State would argue that the statute fits \textit{R.A.V.}'s first exception (like \textit{Virginia v. Black}) if legislative findings show that nationality- and religion-based fighting words on campus carry a uniquely virulent risk of immediate violence, or that the law is evenhanded across all viewpoints (unlike St. Paul's ordinance, it covers ``political ideology'' on all sides). The outcome turns on whether a court sees a demeaning-by-topic rule as a virulent subset of fighting words or as the same topic-based selection \textit{R.A.V.} condemned; the word ``demean'' itself may lead a court to find viewpoint discrimination.
+
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\section{Critique}
+\textbf{Critiques:} Critics (echoing Justices White and Stevens) observe that \textit{R.A.V.} produces a striking paradox: a municipality that bans \textit{all} fighting words suppresses \textit{more} speech yet is constitutionally safer than one targeting only racial slurs, seemingly turning the First Amendment's preference for narrow tailoring upside down. Moreover, the boundary between \textit{R.A.V.}'s first exception (virulent subset of the proscribable harm) and forbidden content discrimination remains notoriously elastic, as demonstrated by the Court's later willingness in \textit{Virginia v. Black} to uphold a targeted cross-burning ban. Defenders counter that \textit{R.A.V.} prevents majorities from weaponizing broad low-value categories to selectively silence disfavored political or social viewpoints.
+
