+\subsection{Concurring Opinion (Justice Barrett)}
 
-\begin{itemize}
-    \item \textbf{Justice Barrett, Concurring:} Joined the majority in full, warning that facial challenges force courts ``to bite off more than [they] can chew'' and urging as-applied suits. She flagged two critical open questions: (1) whether purely user-driven or autonomous AI/LLM moderation attenuates human editorial expression, and (2) whether foreign ownership and control abroad affect a platform's First Amendment rights under \textit{AID v. AOSI}, 591 U.S. 430 (2020) (\href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-277}{Cornell LII}).
-    \item \textbf{Justice Jackson, Concurring in Part and Concurring in the Judgment:} Joined Parts I, II, and III--A, agreeing that the Eleventh Circuit stated First Amendment principles fairly while the Fifth Circuit did not, but declined to join Parts III--B, III--C, and IV under \textit{Ashwander} restraint rather than previewing merits rulings on an undeveloped record (\href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-277}{Cornell LII}).
-    \item \textbf{Justice Thomas, Concurring in the Judgment:} Joined Justice Alito in full and wrote separately to urge reconsideration of \textit{Zauderer}, highlight the common-carrier doctrine for remand, and argue that \emph{all} facial challenges violate Article III's Case-or-Controversy limit by adjudicating nonparties' rights (\href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-277}{Cornell LII}).
