+\section{Memory Jogger}
+Minnesota's revocation-on-divorce statute, applied retroactively, cut an ex-wife out as beneficiary of a life-insurance policy bought before the law passed; an 8--1 Court held there was no Contracts Clause violation because the law did not substantially impair the contract---it tracks likely intent, does what a divorce court could do, and can be undone with a simple form (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/584/16-1432/}{Justia}).
 
-\section{Facts and Procedural History}
+\section{Detailed Case Facts}
 In 1997, Mark Sveen purchased a revocable life-insurance policy from Metropolitan Life Insurance Company (MetLife) and married respondent Kaye Melin later that year. In 1998, Mark named Melin as the policy's primary beneficiary and designated his two adult children from a prior marriage, petitioners Ashley Sveen and Antone Sveen, as contingent beneficiaries.
 
 In 2002, Minnesota amended its probate code to extend its revocation-on-divorce rule from wills to ``will substitutes'' such as life-insurance policies. Codified at Minn. Stat. \S~524.2-804, subd. 1, the statute provides that ``the dissolution or annulment of a marriage revokes any revocable \ldots{} beneficiary designation \ldots{} made by an individual to the individual's former spouse,'' treating the ex-spouse as having predeceased the policyholder unless the policyholder affirmatively re-designates the ex-spouse after divorce or the divorce decree provides otherwise.
 
-Mark Sveen and Kaye Melin divorced in 2007. Their divorce decree did not mention the MetLife policy, and Mark never altered his beneficiary designation. When Mark died in 2011, both Melin and the Sveen children claimed the insurance proceeds. MetLife filed a federal interpleader action in the U.S. District Court for the District of Minnesota to determine the rightful payee.
+Mark Sveen and Kaye Melin divorced in 2007. Their divorce decree did not mention the MetLife policy, and Mark never altered his beneficiary designation. When Mark died in 2011, both Melin and the Sveen children claimed the insurance proceeds.
+
+\section{Procedural History}
+MetLife filed a federal interpleader action in the U.S. District Court for the District of Minnesota to determine the rightful payee.
 
 The District Court granted summary judgment for the Sveen children, holding that retroactive application of Minnesota's 2002 statute revoked Melin's designation and did not violate the Contracts Clause. The U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit reversed (853 F.3d 410 (2017)), holding that retroactively applying a revocation-on-divorce statute to a pre-existing life-insurance designation disrupted the policyholder's expectations and violated the Contracts Clause. To resolve a circuit and state-court split---contrasting the Eighth Circuit and Pennsylvania Supreme Court with the Ninth Circuit, Tenth Circuit, and Colorado Supreme Court---the Supreme Court granted certiorari on December 8, 2017.
 
-\section{Issue(s) Presented}
-Does the retroactive application of Minnesota's revocation-on-divorce statute (Minn. Stat. \S~524.2-804, subd. 1) to a life-insurance beneficiary designation made before the statute's enactment violate the Contracts Clause of Article I, Section 10, Clause 1?
+\section{Judicial Votes}
+The Supreme Court \textbf{reversed and remanded} the Eighth Circuit by an \textbf{8--1} vote. The case was argued on March 19, 2018, and decided on June 11, 2018 (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/584/16-1432/}{Justia}; \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/16-1432}{Cornell LII}):
+
+\begin{itemize}
+    \item \textbf{Majority Opinion:} Justice Kagan delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Roberts and Justices Kennedy, Thomas, Ginsburg, Breyer, Alito, and Sotomayor.
+    \item \textbf{Concurring Opinions:} None.
+    \item \textbf{Dissenting Opinion:} Justice Gorsuch filed a dissenting opinion.
+\end{itemize}
 
 \section{Holding}
-No. Applying the Court's established two-step Contracts Clause framework, retroactive application of Minnesota's revocation-on-divorce statute does not violate the Contracts Clause because the law fails to cross the step-one threshold of ``substantial impairment'' of a pre-existing contractual relationship.
+\textbf{Questions Presented:}
+
+Does the retroactive application of Minnesota's revocation-on-divorce statute (Minn. Stat. \S~524.2-804, subd. 1) to a life-insurance beneficiary designation made before the statute's enactment violate the Contracts Clause of Article I, Section 10, Clause 1?
+
+\textbf{Holding:} No. Applying the Court's established two-step Contracts Clause framework, retroactive application of Minnesota's revocation-on-divorce statute does not violate the Contracts Clause because the law fails to cross the step-one threshold of ``substantial impairment'' of a pre-existing contractual relationship.
+
+\section{Analysis of Opinions}
+\subsection{Majority Opinion (Justice Kagan)}
 
-\section{Reasoning}
 Writing for an eight-Justice majority, Justice Kagan began by reaffirming that while the Contracts Clause restricts state disruption of private contracts, not every state law affecting pre-existing agreements violates the Clause (\textit{El Paso v. Simmons}, 379 U.S. 497, 506--507 (1965)). The Court governs Contracts Clause challenges under a two-step inquiry:
 \begin{enumerate}
   \item \textbf{Threshold Substantial Impairment:} Whether the state law has ``operated as a substantial impairment of a contractual relationship'' (\textit{Allied Structural Steel Co. v. Spannaus}, 438 U.S. 234, 244 (1978)), evaluated by examining the extent to which the law (a) undermines the contractual bargain, (b) interferes with a party's reasonable expectations, and (c) prevents the party from safeguarding or reinstating his or her rights.
@@ -56,53 +65,54 @@ The Court resolved the case entirely at step one---declining in footnote 2 to re
 
 Justice Kagan anchored this third rationale in a two-century line of Contracts Clause precedents upholding retroactive recording, notice, and filing laws---including \textit{Jackson v. Lamphire}, 28 U.S. (3 Pet.) 280 (1830), \textit{Curtis v. Whitney}, 80 U.S. (13 Wall.) 68 (1872), \textit{Vance v. Vance}, 108 U.S. 514 (1883), \textit{Gilfillan v. Union Canal Co. of Pa.}, 109 U.S. 401 (1883), \textit{Conley v. Barton}, 260 U.S. 677 (1923), and \textit{Texaco, Inc. v. Short}, 454 U.S. 516 (1982). Those laws extinguished entire property or debt rights for noncompliance with new filing requirements, whereas Minnesota's statute merely redirects insurance proceeds to contingent beneficiaries. Finally, the Court rejected Melin's proposed distinction between laws that alter substantive contract ``rights'' and laws that regulate procedural ``remedies,'' citing \textit{Texaco} for the rule that when eliminating a remedy and extinguishing a right produce identical practical consequences, the Contracts Clause analysis is the same.
 
-\section{Concurring and Dissenting Opinions}
-\textbf{Justice Gorsuch (Dissenting):} Writing alone, Justice Gorsuch challenged both the doctrinal foundation of the modern balancing test and its application to Minnesota's statute. First, drawing on the text of Article I, Section 10 and Framing-era history (\textit{Sturges v. Crowninshield}, 17 U.S. (4 Wheat.) 122 (1819)), he argued that the Contracts Clause categorically forbids \textit{any} retroactive legislative impairment of existing contracts---not merely ``substantial'' ones---and warned that critics of the post-\textit{Blaisdell} balancing framework ``deserve a thoughtful reply, if not in this case then in another.''
+\subsection{Dissenting Opinion (Justice Gorsuch)}
+
+Writing alone, Justice Gorsuch challenged both the doctrinal foundation of the modern balancing test and its application to Minnesota's statute. First, drawing on the text of Article I, Section 10 and Framing-era history (\textit{Sturges v. Crowninshield}, 17 U.S. (4 Wheat.) 122 (1819)), he argued that the Contracts Clause categorically forbids \textit{any} retroactive legislative impairment of existing contracts---not merely ``substantial'' ones---and warned that critics of the post-\textit{Blaisdell} balancing framework ``deserve a thoughtful reply, if not in this case then in another.''
 
 Second, even under the majority's two-step test, Justice Gorsuch contended that Minnesota's law failed both prongs. At step one, naming the beneficiary is the ``whole point'' of a life-insurance contract; retroactively stripping a named beneficiary alters a core contractual term rather than merely adding a procedural recording step. He dismissed the majority's three justifications: (1) legislatures cannot reliably guess every divorcing spouse's intent (many ex-spouses retain policies for child support or amicable financial security); (2) a judicial divorce decree dividing specific property is fundamentally different from a blanket legislative rewrite of contracts that a divorce court left untouched; and (3) a default rule that catches inattentive policyholders unawares still destroys the contract they actually formed. At step two, he argued that Minnesota had less intrusive, more moderate alternatives---such as requiring divorce courts to notify spouses to review beneficiary forms---without retroactively rewriting pre-existing contracts.
 
+\section{Examples: Future Applications}
+\begin{enumerate}
+
+\item \textbf{Same-Side (No Substantial Impairment): Revocation on Divorce for a Payable-on-Death Account.} In 2010 a depositor opens a bank account payable on his death to his then-wife. In 2015 the State extends its revocation-on-divorce statute to payable-on-death accounts, applying it to existing accounts. The couple divorces in 2018, the depositor never changes the designation, and on his death in 2023 his children and his ex-wife both claim the funds. All three of \textit{Sveen}'s factors favor the State: the statute approximates what most divorced depositors would want, a divorce court could always have reallocated the account, and the depositor could have restored his ex-wife as beneficiary with a simple form. The law does not substantially impair the contract, so the inquiry ends at step one (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/584/16-1432/}{Justia}).
+
+\item \textbf{Same-Side (No Substantial Impairment): A Filing Requirement for Old Mineral Interests.} A State enacts a law providing that a severed mineral interest unused for twenty years lapses and reverts to the surface owner unless the owner files a short statement of claim in the county records within two years after the law's enactment. A mineral owner whose interest predates the statute misses the deadline and loses the interest. \textit{Sveen} relied on a long line of decisions upholding retroactive recording and filing requirements, including \textit{Texaco, Inc.\ v.\ Short}, 454 U.S.\ 516 (1982), which sustained a law of this kind. Because the owner could have preserved the right with minimal paperwork, the law does not substantially impair the contract.
+
+\item \textbf{Opposite-Side (Substantial Impairment): Retroactively Voiding Non-Competes and Guaranties.} Suppose a State enacts a statute retroactively voiding all pre-existing non-compete clauses and personal loan guaranties in commercial leases executed before 2026, with no opt-out or reaffirmation mechanism. \textit{Analysis:} Unlike \textit{Sveen}, this law fails all three step-one factors---it contradicts party intent, shatters core reliance expectations, and permanently bars parties from safeguarding or reinstating their rights. The court must proceed to step two (\textit{Energy Reserves} / \textit{Melendez}) to determine whether the permanent abrogation is reasonable and appropriate to a significant public purpose.
+
+\item \textbf{Opposite-Side (Law Invalid): A State Repeals Its Own Bond Covenant.} To raise money for mass transit, a State repeals a statutory covenant, made to purchasers of its bonds, that limited the use of pledged toll revenues to protect bondholders' security. Bondholders sue. Unlike Minnesota's neutral default rule for private beneficiary designations, the repeal destroys a core term of the bargain, defeats bondholders' reasonable expectations, and cannot be undone by any action of theirs, so it substantially impairs the contract. And because the State is impairing its own financial obligation, the step-two inquiry is not deferential: under \textit{United States Trust Co.\ v.\ New Jersey}, 431 U.S.\ 1 (1977), which struck down a repeal of this kind, the impairment must be reasonable and necessary, and less drastic alternatives were available.
+
+\item \textbf{Fence-Sitter (Uncertain): A Revocation Rule That Is Hard to Undo.} A State's revocation-on-divorce statute applies retroactively to existing life-insurance policies but provides that a policyholder may re-designate a former spouse only by petitioning a court, paying a filing fee, and appearing at a hearing. The first two \textit{Sveen} factors still favor the State: the law reflects probable intent and does what a divorce court could have done. The third factor, however, now cuts the other way, because the policyholder can no longer undo the default ``with the stroke of a pen.'' \textit{Sveen} relied on the three features together and did not say whether the first two suffice alone, so whether this law substantially impairs the contract---and must face step-two review---is genuinely unclear.
+
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\section{Critique}
+\textbf{Notice and Trap-for-the-Unwary Critique:} Justice Kagan's ``stroke of a pen'' rationale assumes policyholders know the legislature retroactively changed their pre-existing contract. An inattentive policyholder who relies on the law as it stood when contracting has no reason to file a new form to preserve an existing designation.
+
+\textbf{Doctrinal Collapse of Step One:} By folding probable intent and legislative reasonableness into step one (``substantial impairment''), \textit{Sveen} allows courts to dismiss Contracts Clause claims at the threshold without ever scrutinizing whether the State chose a narrowly tailored means at step two.
+
+\textbf{Structural Placement Note:} Although placed alongside federal legislative power and economic-regulation limits in this Briefing Book, remember that the Contracts Clause binds \textit{States} (Art. I, \S~10), not Congress---retroactive federal legislation impairing private contracts is instead evaluated under Fifth Amendment Due Process rationality review (or the Takings Clause).
+
