-\section{Doctrinal Significance and Modern Legacy (Through 2026)}
-
-\textit{Fauntleroy v. Lum} stands as the foundational pillar of the Supreme Court's ``iron law'' of Full Faith and Credit for judgments: while states enjoy broad latitude to apply their own substantive law rather than a sister state's statutes (``public Acts''), there is \textbf{no roving ``public policy exception''} to the enforcement of sister-state final judgments.
-
-In the decades immediately following 1908, the Court fortified \textit{Fauntleroy} across the board. In \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/252/411/}{\textit{Kenney v. Supreme Lodge of the World}, 252 U.S. 411 (1920)}, Holmes extended \textit{Fauntleroy} to hold that a state cannot evade Full Faith and Credit by the simple device of denying jurisdiction to its courts over out-of-state wrongful-death judgments. In \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/275/449/}{\textit{Roche v. McDonald}, 275 U.S. 449 (1928)}, and \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/296/268/}{\textit{Milwaukee County v. M. E. White Co.}, 296 U.S. 268 (1935)}, the Court confirmed that \textit{Fauntleroy} bars collateral attacks on both ordinary civil judgments and sister-state tax judgments, expressly disapproving \textit{Pelican}'s broader dictum.
-
-Two modern landmarks cement \textit{Fauntleroy}'s enduring vitality. First, in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/522/222/}{\textit{Baker v. General Motors Corp.}, 522 U.S. 222 (1998)}, Justice Ginsburg reaffirmed \textit{Fauntleroy}'s core rule that the Full Faith and Credit obligation for judgments is ``exacting'' and admits no public-policy exception, even while carving out a narrow limit: one state's equitable anti-suit or anti-testimony decree cannot control the evidentiary machinery of a sister state's courts in litigation involving nonparties. Second, in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/577/464/}{\textit{V.L. v. E.L.}, 577 U.S. 404 (2016)} (per curiam), a unanimous Court summarily reversed the Alabama Supreme Court for refusing to recognize a Georgia second-parent adoption decree, directly quoting Holmes's rule in \textit{Fauntleroy} that courts must be ``slow to read ambiguous words'' in a sister state's statute as jurisdictional rather than merits-based.
-
-Through 2026, \textit{Fauntleroy} sits at the epicenter of the nation's sharpest horizontal-federalism clash: interstate enforcement of judgments arising from post-\textit{Dobbs} abortion ``shield laws.'' After a Texas court entered a \$113,000 default judgment in February 2025 against New York physician Dr. Margaret Carpenter for prescribing abortion medication via telehealth to a Texas patient, a New York county clerk and New York Supreme Court invoked New York's shield law to refuse docketing the Texas judgment (\href{https://journals.law.umn.edu/mjlst/2026/11/10/telehealth-abortion-and-the-limits-of-state-shield-laws}{\textit{Minnesota Journal of Law, Science \& Technology}, Nov. 10, 2026}). In September 2026, Louisiana, Arkansas, and Alabama moved for leave to file an original-jurisdiction bill of complaint in the U.S. Supreme Court against New York, Massachusetts, and California challenging telehealth shield laws (\href{https://www.ag.state.la.us/Files/Article/637/Documents/asfiledLouisianav.NewYork-Complaint.pdf}{\textit{Louisiana v. New York} Complaint, 2026}). Under \textit{Fauntleroy}, shield-law states cannot rely on a generic public-policy defense; instead, litigation through late 2026 turns on the exact two escape valves Holmes left open---whether the rendering state lacked personal jurisdiction over the out-of-state provider and whether statutory civil fines or bounty awards fall within the ``penal judgment'' exception recognized in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/146/657/}{\textit{Huntington v. Attrill}, 146 U.S. 657 (1892)}.
-
-\section{Pedagogical Critiques and Exam Hypotheticals}
-
-\subsection{Critiques}
-\begin{itemize}
-    \item \textbf{Forum-Shopping and Transient Jurisdiction:} Critics note that \textit{Fauntleroy} incentivizes strategic ``tag'' jurisdiction (serving a defendant passing through a favorable forum) to launder a claim barred in the parties' home state into an unimpeachable sister-state judgment.
-    \item \textbf{Asymmetry Between Laws and Judgments:} Modern doctrine creates a stark cliff: before judgment, Mississippi was free to apply its own anti-gambling statute; the instant Missouri entered judgment---even by glaring legal error---Mississippi's sovereign policy evaporated.
-    \item \textbf{Holmes's Optimism Tested:} Holmes predicted that deliberate sister-state overrides of another state's criminal or public-policy laws would be ``rare.'' Modern culture-war federalism (reproductive care, firearms liability, and non-compete bans) directly tests that assumption.
-\end{itemize}
-
-\subsection{Exam Hypothetical and ``Opposite Side'' Contrast}
-\textbf{Hypothetical (Public Policy Versus Penal and Jurisdictional Exceptions):} State A prohibits sports-betting debts as a misdemeanor and declares them ``unenforceable in any court.'' Resident X and Resident Y, both domiciled in State A, enter an illegal wager in State A. While Y attends a trade show in State B, X serves Y and obtains a final civil money judgment in State B's trial court, which mistakenly rejects Y's State A illegality defense. Y does not appeal in State B. When X seeks to enforce the judgment in State A, State A's courts refuse on public-policy grounds. \textit{Analysis:} Under \textit{Fauntleroy}, State A \textbf{must} enforce the judgment. State B had valid personal jurisdiction (in-state service) and subject-matter jurisdiction; its mistake of State A law had to be corrected on direct appeal in State B, not collaterally in State A.
-
-\textbf{``Opposite Side'' Contrast:} Now alter the facts so that \textit{State B's Attorney General} obtains a \$100,000 statutory civil penalty in State B against Y (a State A resident who never entered State B and never appeared) for mailing regulated goods into State B, and seeks enforcement in State A. Here, State A has two potent \textit{Fauntleroy}-compatible defenses that flip the outcome: (1) \textit{lack of personal jurisdiction} in State B (which Holmes expressly preserved as a valid collateral attack, 210 U.S. at 237, and which Y did not waive by defaulting), and (2) the \textit{penal-judgment exception} (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/146/657/}{\textit{Huntington v. Attrill}, 146 U.S. 657 (1892)}), arguing that a state-brought punitive fine---unlike Fauntleroy's private contract/arbitration recovery---is a penal obligation that sister states need not execute. A third contrast arises under \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/522/222/}{\textit{Baker v. General Motors Corp.}, 522 U.S. 222 (1998)}: if State B enters an injunction barring Y from testifying in State A on behalf of nonparty strangers, State A may disregard the order because one state's decree cannot hijack another state's evidentiary procedures against nonparties.
 
