-To test the narrow outer boundary of what still survives \textit{Abbasi}, contrast \textit{Abbasi} with \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/403/388/}{\textit{Bivens}}, 403 U.S. 388 (1971), and the Seventh Circuit's post-\textit{Egbert} application in \href{https://media.ca7.uscourts.gov/cgi-bin/OpinionsWeb/processWebInputExternal.pl?Submit=Display&Path=Y2023/D06-27/C:21-1463:J:Sykes:aut:T:fnOp:N:3066917:S:0}{\textit{Snowden v. Henning}}, 72 F.4th 237 (7th Cir. 2023). In \textit{Snowden}, a DEA agent allegedly used excessive force while executing a domestic arrest warrant at a hotel. Applying \textit{Abbasi}'s ``meaningful difference'' factors, the Seventh Circuit allowed the Fourth Amendment claim to proceed because a line-level narcotics officer making a routine domestic arrest implicated neither national security, high-level policy, nor a new category of defendants---demonstrating that only claims closely tracking \textit{Bivens}'s core domestic law-enforcement facts avoid Step One dismissal.
