 \end{quote}
+\noindent --- Justice White, dissenting, \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/434/452}{434 U.S. at 482}.
 
-\section{Doctrinal Significance and Modern Legacy (Through 2026)}
-\textit{U.S. Steel} is the cornerstone of modern Compact Clause jurisprudence and horizontal federalism. Together with two closely linked successors, it defines the constitutional architecture of interstate cooperation:
-\begin{itemize}
-  \item \textbf{Clarifying Consent and ``Law of the Union'' Status:} In \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/449/433/}{\textit{Cuyler v. Adams}}, 449 U.S. 433 (1981), the Court held that when Congress \textit{does} consent to an interstate compact on a subject appropriate for federal legislation, congressional consent transforms the compact into federal law, making its construction a federal question. In \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/472/159/}{\textit{Northeast Bancorp, Inc. v. Board of Governors}}, 472 U.S. 159 (1985), the Court applied \textit{U.S. Steel} to uphold regional New England banking statutes, refining the indicia of a ``compact'' (such as a joint organization, non-unilateral withdrawal, and reciprocal obligations) while confirming that even if an agreement existed, it neither enhanced state power over the Federal Government nor impaired nonmember states' sovereignty.
-  \item \textbf{Expansion of Unconsented Interstate Cooperation:} By insulating non-sovereignty-delegating interstate bodies from congressional veto, \textit{U.S. Steel} enabled a vast modern network of more than 260 active interstate compacts spanning occupational licensing (such as the Interstate Teacher Mobility and Social Work Licensure Compacts), tax administration, insurance regulation, and regional policy coordination (\href{https://www.americanbar.org/groups/state_local_government/resources/urban-lawyer/52-3/developments-interstate-compact-law-practice-2023/}{ABA Urban Lawyer}). Notably, the Supreme Court has \textit{never} invalidated an interstate compact solely for lack of congressional consent (\href{https://nyujlpp.org/quorum/langsam-compact-clause-national-popular-vote/}{NYU J. Legis. \& Pub. Pol'y Quorum 2025}).
-  \item \textbf{The National Popular Vote Interstate Compact (NPVIC) Debate Through 2026:} \textit{U.S. Steel} sits at the center of the highest-stakes Compact Clause controversy of the 2020s: the NPVIC, under which participating states pledge their Electoral College votes to the national popular vote winner once states totaling 270 electoral votes join. As of 2026, 17--18 states plus the District of Columbia (totaling roughly 209--222 electoral votes depending on post-2024 enactments) have adopted the NPVIC, with additional state bills active ahead of potential activation (\href{https://nyujlpp.org/quorum/langsam-compact-clause-national-popular-vote/}{NYU J. Legis. \& Pub. Pol'y Quorum 2025}). Supporters invoke \textit{U.S. Steel} to argue that each state already possesses plenary Article II power over elector appointment and that the NPVIC does not encroach on federal supremacy; critics invoke Justice White's dissent and \textit{Northeast Bancorp}'s reference to sister states to argue that an unconsented 270-vote bloc coercively neutralizes nonmember states' electoral influence and alters the national constitutional structure.
-\end{itemize}
-
-\section{Pedagogical Critiques and Exam Hypotheticals}
-\subsection{Key Critiques}
-\begin{itemize}
-  \item \textbf{Nullification of Constitutional Text:} Originalist and textualist critics echo Justice White: \textit{U.S. Steel} reads ``any Agreement or Compact'' so narrowly that virtually no domestic interstate arrangement ever requires consent unless it already violates another constitutional limit.
-  \item \textbf{Blindness to Horizontal Federalism:} By testing state power exclusively \textit{quoad} the Federal Government, Powell's opinion gives short shrift to the danger that a regional cartel of states can impose political or economic externalities on excluded sister states without directly usurping a federal statute.
-  \item \textbf{Underestimating Institutional Synergy:} Treating a permanent, staffed multistate agency as constitutionally identical to 21 states acting independently ignores collective-action dynamics and coordinated lobbying power.
-\end{itemize}
-
-\subsection{Exam Hypotheticals and ``Opposite Side'' Contrast}
-\textbf{Hypothetical 1 (Unconsented Interstate AI Safety Commission):} Fifteen states enact an unconsented ``Interstate AI Oversight Compact'' creating a joint commission that drafts model algorithmic-transparency rules and conducts voluntary joint technical audits of AI companies at a member state's request. Member states may accept or reject any model rule, impose their own fines, and withdraw upon 30 days' notice. \textit{Analysis:} Under \textit{U.S. Steel}, the compact is valid without congressional consent. Even though Congress has debated federal AI legislation (a ``federal interest''), the compact delegates no sovereign authority, exercises no power unavailable to individual states, permits unilateral withdrawal, and leaves Congress's Commerce Clause preemption power intact.
-
-\textbf{Hypothetical 2 (``Opposite Side'' Contrast --- Triggering the Compact Clause):} Suppose instead twelve coastal states form an unconsented ``Atlantic Maritime Security Compact'' that establishes a binding regional port authority with independent power to levy harbor tariffs on foreign vessels, negotiate bilateral customs-inspection agreements with European nations, and prohibit any member state from withdrawing for 25 years without unanimous consent. \textit{Contrast:} Unlike the Multistate Tax Compact in \textit{U.S. Steel}, this pact violates the Compact Clause (and the Treaty and Import--Export/Tonnage Clauses). It binds member states irrevocably, delegates sovereign taxing and enforcement powers to an interstate body that no single state could lawfully exercise alone, and directly encroaches upon exclusive federal supremacy over foreign affairs and foreign commerce (\href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution-conan/article-1/section-1/clause-3/requirement-of-congressional-consent-to-compacts}{Cornell LII Constitution Annotated}). Under \textit{Virginia v. Tennessee} and \textit{U.S. Steel}, such an agreement cannot operate without express congressional consent.
 
