-\section{Doctrinal Significance and Modern Legacy (Through 2026)}
-\textit{Boumediene v. Bush} remains a landmark assertion of judicial supremacy over congressional jurisdiction-stripping and executive wartime detention---marking the first time the Supreme Court invalidated a federal statute under the Suspension Clause. Yet its subsequent trajectory through 2026 reveals a markedly narrowed footprint:
-\begin{itemize}
-  \item \textbf{Limits on Extraterritorial Reach:} Applying \textit{Boumediene}'s three-factor test, the D.C. Circuit held in \href{https://caselaw.findlaw.com/court/us-dc-circuit/1524965.html}{\textit{Al Maqaleh v. Gates}, 605 F.3d 84 (D.C. Cir. 2010)} (and reaffirmed in \href{https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/cadc/12-5404/12-5404-2013-12-24.html}{\textit{Al Maqaleh v. Hagel}, 738 F.3d 312 (D.C. Cir. 2013)}), that the Suspension Clause did \textit{not} extend to noncitizen detainees held at Bagram Airfield in Afghanistan because Bagram lay inside an active theater of war where the United States lacked Guantanamo's century-long, exclusive control.
-  \item \textbf{Deferential Post-\textit{Boumediene} Guantanamo Review:} On remand, Lakhdar Boumediene won his habeas petition and was released to France in 2009. Over the next decade, however, the D.C. Circuit adopted deferential preponderance-of-the-evidence and hearsay standards for AUMF detentions and declined to extend the Fifth Amendment Due Process Clause broadly to Guantanamo detainees (see \href{https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/cadc/19-5079/19-5079-2020-08-28.html}{\textit{Al-Hela v. Trump}, 972 F.3d 120 (D.C. Cir. 2020)}).
-  \item \textbf{Cabining the Suspension Clause in Immigration Law (\textit{Thuraissigiam}):} In \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/19-161}{\textit{Department of Homeland Security v. Thuraissigiam}, 591 U.S. 103 (2020)} (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/591/19-161/}{Justia}), the Supreme Court (7--2 on the Suspension Clause) sharply limited \textit{Boumediene}'s expansionist potential. Upholding 8 U.S.C. \S~1252(e)(2)'s bar on habeas review of expedited-removal credible-fear determinations, Justice Alito held that the Suspension Clause protects only the writ ``as it existed in 1789''---a remedy to secure \textit{release from unlawful executive custody}, not a vehicle to obtain asylum or lawful admission into the United States.
-  \item \textbf{Habeas and Custody Challenges Through 2026:} In 2025--2026 litigation over mandatory immigration detention and extraterritorial removals, federal courts have treated \textit{Boumediene} and \textit{Thuraissigiam} as twin poles: noncitizens seeking admission remain barred under \textit{Thuraissigiam}, whereas individuals challenging the legality of ongoing executive \textit{detention} itself continue to invoke core habeas jurisdiction under the Suspension Clause (see, e.g., \href{https://iptp-production.s3.amazonaws.com/media/documents/2026.02.26_Order_Granting_Writ_of_Habeas_Corpus_-_Longoria_Mendoza_v._Noem.pdf}{\textit{Longoria Mendoza v. Noem} (W.D. Tex. Feb. 26, 2026)}).
-\end{itemize}
-
-\section{Pedagogical Critiques and Exam Hypotheticals}
-\subsection{Pedagogical Critiques}
-\begin{itemize}
-  \item \textbf{Indeterminacy of the Functional Test:} Justice Kennedy's three-factor test avoids formalistic evasion of the Constitution, but it provides lower courts little bright-line guidance on when U.S. military or diplomatic control over a foreign site crosses the threshold into \textit{de facto} sovereignty.
-  \item \textbf{Remedy Without Substantive Rules:} \textit{Boumediene} guaranteed Guantanamo detainees access to federal habeas courts but deliberately left open the substantive definition of ``enemy combatant,'' the burden of proof, and evidentiary rules---inviting years of D.C. Circuit litigation that ultimately narrowed detainee victories.
-  \item \textbf{Tension with \textit{Thuraissigiam}:} Compare \textit{Boumediene}'s living-functionalist approach to the Suspension Clause (rejecting 1789 territorial formalism as inconclusive) with \textit{Thuraissigiam}'s strict 1789 originalism---an acute methodological shift reflecting the Court's changed composition between 2008 and 2020.
-\end{itemize}
-
-\subsection{Exam Hypotheticals and ``Opposite Side'' Contrast}
-\begin{itemize}
-  \item \textbf{Hypothetical 1 (Permanent Overseas Detention Base Outside an Active War Zone):} Suppose the United States signs a 99-year lease with a Pacific island nation granting ``exclusive U.S. administrative control'' over a remote atoll used to detain noncitizens suspected of cyber-terrorism, and Congress strips federal habeas jurisdiction while substituting an internal military review board that bars counsel and newly discovered exculpatory evidence. \textit{Analysis:} Under \textit{Boumediene}'s three factors---(1) contested status with weak process, (2) long-term exclusive \textit{de facto} U.S. control outside an active war zone, and (3) minimal practical obstacles---the Suspension Clause applies, and the jurisdiction-stripping statute is an unconstitutional suspension unless Congress invokes Rebellion or Invasion or provides an adequate judicial substitute.
-  \item \textbf{Hypothetical 2 (``Opposite Side'' Contrast --- Active Combat Zone or Initial-Entry Immigration Removal):} Contrast \textit{Boumediene} with two ``opposite side'' scenarios where a Suspension Clause challenge fails: (1) \textbf{Active Theater of War (\textit{Eisentrager} / \textit{Al Maqaleh}):} A foreign national captured and held by U.S. forces at a temporary forward operating base inside an active foreign combat zone lacks a constitutional habeas right because the United States lacks permanent exclusive control and judicial review would create severe practical and military obstacles. (2) \textbf{Expedited Removal at the Border (\textit{Thuraissigiam}):} A noncitizen apprehended 25 yards inside the U.S. border who seeks habeas review not to obtain release from custody, but to compel a second asylum hearing and remain in the country, cannot invoke \textit{Boumediene} because such relief falls outside the historical 1789 core of the writ.
-\end{itemize}
 
