-\section{Doctrinal Significance and Modern Legacy (Through 2026)}
-\textit{Laidlaw} occupies a bifurcated legacy in modern constitutional law through 2026: its voluntary-cessation mootness rule remains black-letter, unanimous Supreme Court orthodoxy, whereas its relatively generous approach to standing and statutory citizen suits has come under sustained pressure from the Roberts Court's stricter Article III jurisprudence.
-\begin{itemize}
-  \item \textbf{Enduring Authority on Voluntary Cessation (\textit{Already} and \textit{FBI v. Fikre}):} The Court repeatedly reaffirmed \textit{Laidlaw}'s ``formidable burden'' for voluntary cessation---first in \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/11-982}{\textit{Already, LLC v. Nike, Inc.}, 568 U.S. 85 (2013)} (finding mootness only where Nike issued an unconditional, irrevocable covenant not to sue), and most prominently in \href{https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/23pdf/601us1r11_7lh8.pdf}{\textit{FBI v. Fikre}, 601 U.S. 234 (2024)} (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/601/22-1178/}{Justia}). In a unanimous opinion by Justice Gorsuch, \textit{Fikre} relied directly on \textit{Laidlaw} (528 U.S. at 189) to hold that the federal government's post-suit removal of a U.S. citizen from the No Fly List did not moot his due-process suit because a bare declaration promising not to relist him based on ``currently available information'' failed to prove the challenged conduct could not reasonably be expected to recur.
-  \item \textbf{Tightening Concrete Injury and Statutory Standing (\textit{TransUnion} and Beyond):} By contrast, \textit{Laidlaw}'s expansive view of injury and citizen-suit redressability stands in growing tension with \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/20-297}{\textit{TransUnion LLC v. Ramirez}, 594 U.S. 413 (2021)} (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/594/20-297/}{Justia}). Echoing Justice Scalia's \textit{Laidlaw} dissent, \textit{TransUnion} held that Congress cannot create Article III injury merely by enacting a statutory cause of action; plaintiffs must show concrete harm bearing a ``close historical or common-law analogue,'' and unmaterialized risk of future harm cannot support retrospective damages. Through 2026, the Court has continued to police concrete aesthetic and organizational injuries rigorously---for instance, staying an injunction in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/609/26a203/}{\textit{National Park Service v. National Trust for Historic Preservation}, 609 U.S. \_\_\_ (2026)}, on the ground that mere distress at viewing a building's design does not constitute a concrete Article III injury.
-\end{itemize}
-
-\section{Pedagogical Critiques and Exam Hypotheticals}
-\subsection{Pedagogical Critiques}
-\begin{itemize}
-  \item \textbf{Reasonable Fear vs. Objective Harm:} Critics echo Justice Scalia's concern that separating ``injury to the plaintiff'' from ``injury to the environment'' risks manufacturing standing from subjective anxiety whenever a defendant violates a technical regulatory threshold, even when the violation causes zero physical or ecological harm.
