+\section{Memory Jogger}
+Overruling \textit{Chevron}, the Court held 6--3 (6--2 in \textit{Loper Bright}) in a challenge to a rule making herring fishermen pay for federal observers that the APA requires courts to exercise independent judgment on whether an agency acted within its statutory authority, rather than deferring to an agency's reading of an ambiguous statute (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/603/22-451/}{Justia}).
 
+\section{Detailed Case Facts}
 To curb overfishing off the U.S. coast, Congress enacted the Magnuson-Stevens Fishery Conservation and Management Act (MSA), 16 U.S.C. \S~1801 \textit{et seq.}, administered by the National Marine Fisheries Service (NMFS) under the Secretary of Commerce. Under the MSA, regional fishery management plans may require domestic vessels to carry onboard federal ``observers'' to collect conservation data (\S~1853(b)(8)). The statute expressly authorizes industry-funded observer programs for three specific groups---foreign fishing vessels, vessels in limited access privilege programs, and vessels in the North Pacific Council (capping fees at 2--3\% of harvest value for the latter two)---but is silent on whether Atlantic herring fishermen must pay for observers (\href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-451}{603 U.S. at 380--381}).
 
 In 2020, NMFS promulgated a final rule (85 Fed. Reg. 7414) approving an industry-funded monitoring program for the Atlantic herring fishery targeting 50\% trip coverage. Whenever NMFS required an observer but declined to supply a government-funded monitor, vessel owners had to contract with and pay certified third-party observers---estimated by NMFS at up to \$710 per day, reducing annual vessel returns by up to 20\% (\textit{id.} at 381).
 
+\section{Procedural History}
 Two sets of family-owned Atlantic herring fishing businesses sued under the MSA (which incorporates the Administrative Procedure Act (APA), 5 U.S.C. \S~551 \textit{et seq.}), arguing the MSA did not authorize NMFS to shift observer salaries onto domestic fishermen. In \textit{Loper Bright}, the District Court granted summary judgment to the Government (544 F. Supp. 3d 82 (D.D.C. 2021)), and a divided D.C. Circuit affirmed (45 F.4th 359 (2022)), finding the MSA not ``wholly unambiguous'' and deferring to NMFS as ``reasonable'' under Step Two of \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/467/837/}{\textit{Chevron U.S.A. Inc. v. Natural Resources Defense Council, Inc.}}, 467 U.S. 837 (1984) (Judge Walker dissenting). In \textit{Relentless}, the District of Rhode Island (561 F. Supp. 3d 226 (2021)) and the First Circuit (62 F.4th 621 (2023)) likewise upheld the rule under \textit{Chevron}, though the First Circuit declined to specify whether its holding rested on Step One or Step Two. The Supreme Court granted certiorari in both cases limited to whether \textit{Chevron} should be overruled or clarified.
 
