-\end{quote}
+\subsection{Subsequent Developments}
 
-\section{Doctrinal Significance and Modern Legacy (Through 2026)}
+\begin{itemize}
 
-\textit{Loper Bright} reshaped American administrative law and separation-of-powers litigation across federal courts through 2026:
+\item \textbf{Clarified Boundaries in 2025--2026 Supreme Court Decisions:} The Court has simultaneously checked overbroad readings of \textit{Loper Bright}. In \href{https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/24pdf/23-975_m648.pdf}{\textit{Seven County Infrastructure Coalition v. Eagle County}}, 605 U.S. \_\_\_ (2025), the Court distinguished pure statutory interpretation under \textit{Loper Bright} from substantial judicial deference owed to agency technical and factual judgment in NEPA environmental reviews. Likewise, in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-777/}{\textit{Urias-Orellana v. Bondi}}, 607 U.S. \_\_\_ (2026), the Court confirmed that \textit{Loper Bright} does not displace express statutory deferential review standards (such as INA \S~1252(b)(4)(B)) or lawful delegations of agency authority.
 
-\begin{itemize}
-  \item \textbf{Amplification via \textit{Corner Post} and Circuit Invalidations:} Decided three days later, \href{https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/23pdf/22-1008_1b82.pdf}{\textit{Corner Post, Inc. v. Board of Governors}}, 603 U.S. 799 (2024), held that the six-year APA limitations period begins when a plaintiff is injured rather than when a rule is promulgated, exposing older regulations to fresh \textit{Loper Bright} review. Applying \textit{Loper Bright} without \textit{Chevron} deference, the Sixth Circuit in \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/net_neutrality}{\textit{Ohio Telecom Ass'n v. FCC}} (\textit{In re MCP No. 185}), 124 F.4th 993 (6th Cir. 2025), vacated the FCC's 2024 Title II net-neutrality reclassification of broadband internet service.
