-\section{Pedagogical Critiques and Exam Hypotheticals}
+\section{Examples: Future Applications}
+\begin{enumerate}
 
-\subsection{Critiques}
+\item \textbf{Same-Side (No Deference): Defining a Statutory Term.} Suppose Congress empowers the EPA to set emissions limits for ``emerging synthetic compounds'' and directs the agency to promulgate rules ``as the Administrator deems reasonable and necessary to protect public health.'' EPA defines ``emerging synthetic compound'' to include a legacy chemical used since 1950. Whether a chemical in use for seventy years is ``emerging'' is a question about the scope of EPA's statutory authority, not a choice within it. Under \textit{Loper Bright}, the court reviews the definition \textit{de novo} using traditional statutory tools (granting at most \textit{Skidmore} persuasive weight), and if the best reading of ``emerging'' excludes legacy chemicals, EPA's definition falls even though the term is ambiguous.
 
-\begin{itemize}
-  \item \textbf{The ``Single Best Meaning'' Fiction:} Critics echo Justice Kagan's argument that many technical regulatory phrases genuinely lack a single textually fixed meaning at enactment, meaning \textit{Loper Bright} transfers unavoidable policy choices from accountable agencies to life-tenured judges---risking circuit splits and ideological divergence.
-  \item \textbf{Boundary Uncertainty Over ``Delegations'':} Because Roberts preserved deference where a statute delegates discretion (e.g., ``appropriate'' or ``reasonable''), scholars note that disputes once fought at \textit{Chevron} Step One have migrated into an equally contested battle over which statutory terms qualify as express delegations.
-  \item \textbf{Tension with Statutory \textit{Stare Decisis}:} Lower courts and litigants struggle to reconcile \textit{Loper Bright}'s assurance that prior \textit{Chevron} holdings remain intact with agencies' attempts to revise old rules---since an agency can no longer rely on \textit{Brand X} to switch from one ``permissible'' reading to another.
-\end{itemize}
+\item \textbf{Same-Side (No Deference): Making Regulated Parties Pay for Inspectors.} A statute authorizes an agency to require inspectors aboard cargo barges and expressly allows the agency to charge barge operators for inspectors in two specified programs, but says nothing about charging operators in other programs. The agency issues a rule requiring all operators to pay for inspectors. Before \textit{Loper Bright}, a court might have found the silence ambiguous and upheld the rule as a permissible reading. Now the court must decide for itself the ``single, best meaning'' of the statute, and Congress's express authorization of fees in only two programs strongly suggests that the agency has no such power elsewhere. The agency's view receives at most \textit{Skidmore} respect (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/603/22-451/}{Justia}).
 
-\subsection{``Opposite Side'' Contrast: \textit{Chevron} and \textit{Seven County Infrastructure Coalition}}
+\item \textbf{Opposite-Side (Deference Survives): A Choice Within an Express Delegation.} Under the same emerging-compounds statute, EPA chooses a 10-parts-per-billion threshold after weighing scientific studies and compliance costs. Here the court recognizes Congress's express ``reasonable and necessary'' delegation and reviews EPA's threshold deferentially for reasoned decisionmaking under APA \S~706(2)(A). \textit{Loper Bright} expressly preserved respect for statutes that delegate discretionary authority to an agency; the court polices the boundary of the delegation but does not substitute its own judgment about the right number.
 
-Contrast \textit{Loper Bright} with \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/467/837/}{\textit{Chevron U.S.A. Inc. v. NRDC}}, 467 U.S. 837 (1984), and \href{https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/24pdf/23-975_m648.pdf}{\textit{Seven County Infrastructure Coalition v. Eagle County}}, 605 U.S. \_\_\_ (2025). In \textit{Chevron}, statutory silence on whether ``stationary source'' meant a single machine or an entire plant triggered mandatory judicial deference to the EPA's policy accommodation. Under \textit{Loper Bright}, that same silence triggers independent judicial construction to find the single best reading. Yet \textit{Seven County} (and \textit{Urias-Orellana}, 2026) illustrates the opposite side of \textit{Loper Bright} itself: once a court independently confirms the statute's boundaries, agencies still receive substantial deference on factual findings, technical predictions, and reasoned policy choices within expressly delegated discretion.
+\item \textbf{Opposite-Side (Prior Holding Stands): Relitigating a Rule Upheld Under \textit{Chevron}.} In 2010 the Supreme Court upheld a specific agency rule, applying \textit{Chevron} and deferring to the agency's reading of an ambiguous statute. After \textit{Loper Bright}, a company subject to the same rule sues, arguing that the rule must now be vacated because the precedent upholding it rested on the overruled deference framework. The challenge fails. \textit{Loper Bright} stated that it does not call into question prior cases that relied on \textit{Chevron}: their holdings that specific agency actions are lawful remain subject to statutory \textit{stare decisis}, and mere reliance on \textit{Chevron} is not a ``special justification'' for overruling them.
 
-\subsection{Exam Hypotheticals}
+\item \textbf{Fence-Sitter (Uncertain): An Agency Reverses Course.} In 2012, a court of appeals upheld an FDA rule under \textit{Chevron} Step Two. In 2026, a new administration repeals that rule and adopts the opposite statutory interpretation. Under \textit{Loper Bright} (603 U.S. at 412), statutory \textit{stare decisis} protected the 2012 holding that the original rule was lawful---but without \textit{Chevron} and \textit{Brand X}, can the agency defend its new interpretation as merely ``permissible,'' or must the reviewing court now independently determine the single best meaning of the statute? The 2012 decision held only that the old reading was permissible, not that it was the best reading, so it may not settle whether the opposite reading is lawful. If the court concludes the old reading was best, the reversal fails; if the new reading is best, the prior precedent may not stand in its way. \textit{Loper Bright} did not say how such a precedent binds a later, contrary interpretation.
 
-\begin{enumerate}
-  \item \textbf{Express Delegation vs. Statutory Silence:} Congress empowers the EPA to set emissions limits for ``emerging synthetic compounds'' and directs the agency to promulgate rules ``as the Administrator deems reasonable and necessary to protect public health.'' EPA (a) defines ``emerging synthetic compound'' to include a legacy chemical used since 1950, and (b) chooses a 10-parts-per-billion threshold after weighing scientific studies and compliance costs. Under \textit{Loper Bright}, a court reviews (a) \textit{de novo} using traditional statutory tools (granting at most \textit{Skidmore} persuasive weight), whereas under (b) the court recognizes Congress's express ``reasonable and necessary'' delegation and reviews EPA's threshold deferentially for reasoned decisionmaking under APA \S~706(2)(A).
-  \item \textbf{Post-\textit{Loper Bright} Agency Flip-Flop:} In 2012, a court of appeals upheld an FDA rule under \textit{Chevron} Step Two. In 2026, a new administration repeals that rule and adopts the opposite statutory interpretation. Under \textit{Loper Bright} (603 U.S. at 412), statutory \textit{stare decisis} protected the 2012 holding that the original rule was lawful---but without \textit{Chevron} and \textit{Brand X}, can the agency defend its new interpretation as merely ``permissible,'' or must the reviewing court now independently determine the single best meaning of the statute?
 \end{enumerate}
 
