-\section{Doctrinal Significance and Modern Legacy (Through 2026)}
-\textit{Comstock} became the modern touchstone for the Necessary and Proper Clause, yet subsequent Supreme Court decisions immediately cabined its most expansive implications (\href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution-conan/article-1/section-8/clause-1/modern-necessary-and-proper-clause-doctrine}{Constitution Annotated}). Two years later, in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/567/519/}{\textit{National Federation of Independent Business v. Sebelius}}, 567 U.S. 519 (2012), Chief Justice Roberts---who had joined Breyer's majority in \textit{Comstock}---joined by the four joint dissenters on this point, rejected the federal government's attempt to sustain the Affordable Care Act's individual health-insurance mandate under the Necessary and Proper Clause. Roberts sharply distinguished \textit{Comstock} as an exercise of authority ``derivative of, and in service to, a granted power,'' holding that even if the mandate was ``necessary,'' compelling inactive individuals to enter commerce to create the predicate for regulation was not ``proper.''
-
-One year later, however, the Court reaffirmed \textit{Comstock}'s core framework in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/570/387/}{\textit{United States v. Kebodeaux}}, 570 U.S. 387 (2013), holding 7--2 (again via Justice Breyer) that the Military Regulation Clause combined with the Necessary and Proper Clause empowered Congress to apply federal sex-offender registration requirements (SORNA) to a former Air Force serviceman who had already completed his court-martial sentence, because he remained subject to a preexisting federal registration obligation. Chief Justice Roberts and Justice Alito concurred in the judgment to stress that Congress lacks a general federal police power.
-
-Through 2026, the Supreme Court continues to treat \textit{Comstock} and \textit{Sabri} as bounded rather than plenary grants of implied power. For example, in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/609/23-1197/}{\textit{Landor v. Louisiana Department of Corrections and Public Safety}}, 609 U.S. \_\_\_ (2026), the Court cited \textit{Comstock}'s reading of \textit{Sabri} while refusing to infer a private damages remedy against nonconsenting state prison officials under RLUIPA, underscoring that incidental powers under the Necessary and Proper Clause must remain tightly tethered to safeguarding an enumerated federal power without eroding state sovereignty.
-
-\section{Pedagogical Critiques and Exam Hypotheticals}
-\textbf{Pedagogical Critique:} \textit{Comstock} exposes a deep methodological fault line in structural constitutional law. Breyer's five-factor ``taken together'' test is pragmatic and flexible, but critics echo Justice Thomas's objection: if Congress can stack implied power upon implied power (enumerated power $\rightarrow$ federal crime $\rightarrow$ federal prison $\rightarrow$ prison medical care $\rightarrow$ post-sentence civil commitment), what principled stopping point remains? By contrast, Kennedy and Alito's concurrences---and Roberts's later opinion in \textit{NFIB}---recast ``proper'' as an independent federalism check preventing Congress from converting incidental custodial powers into a general police power.
-
-\textbf{``Opposite Side'' Contrast (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/567/519/}{\textit{NFIB v. Sebelius}}, 567 U.S. 519 (2012)):} Pair \textit{Comstock} with \textit{NFIB} to illustrate the boundary between ``necessary'' and ``proper.'' In \textit{Comstock}, Congress regulated a narrow class of individuals already lawfully within federal prison custody, and \S~4248 operated as a derivative incident of running a federal correctional system without displacing state civil-commitment authority. In \textit{NFIB}, by contrast, the individual mandate did not regulate persons already subject to a federal program; it compelled uninsured citizens to enter interstate commerce in order to make Congress's broader insurance regulations work. Under \textit{NFIB}, a measure that creates its own regulatory predicate rather than carrying an existing enumerated power into execution is not ``proper,'' even if it satisfies \textit{Comstock}'s means-ends rationality prong.
-
-\textbf{Exam Hypothetical:} Congress enacts a statute authorizing federal courts to impose lifetime federal civil commitment on any person who has ever been convicted of a \emph{state} violent felony if the person once purchased a firearm that traveled in interstate commerce, even if the person was never in federal custody and the state declines to commit him. A challenger attacks the statute as exceeding Article I. How should a court rule? \textit{Analysis:} Under \textit{Comstock}, \textit{Kebodeaux}, and \textit{NFIB}, the statute is unconstitutional. Unlike \S~4248 in \textit{Comstock}, the law is not tied to existing federal custody or responsible management of federal prisons, lacks any historical pedigree of federal supervision over state-only offenders, intrudes directly into the states' traditional police power, and uses an attenuated past commercial nexus to manufacture a sweeping federal civil-commitment power that is neither ``substantially linked'' (Alito/Kennedy) nor ``proper'' (\textit{NFIB}).
 
 \end{document}
