+\section{Examples: Future Applications}
+\begin{enumerate}
+
+\item \textbf{Same-Side (State Lacks Power): Excluding a U.S.\ Senate Candidate.} Suppose in 2028 a State Secretary of State excludes a former U.S.\ Senator from the U.S.\ Senate primary ballot after a state administrative hearing finds the candidate ``gave aid or comfort'' to a domestic rebellion, while simultaneously barring a former county sheriff from running for Governor on the exact same factual record. Consider first the Senate candidate. Under \emph{Trump v. Anderson}, the exclusion from the \emph{U.S.\ Senate} ballot is unconstitutional because States lack authority to enforce Section~3 against federal candidates; enforcement belongs exclusively to Congress (via Section~5 legislation or the Senate's Article~I, \S 5 judging power).
 
+\item \textbf{Same-Side (State Lacks Power): A Voter Challenge to a Sitting Representative.} Voters in a congressional district petition a state court under the state election code to remove their incumbent U.S.\ Representative from the general-election ballot, presenting evidence that he helped plan an armed attack on a federal building. The state court finds that he engaged in insurrection and orders him off the ballot. Under \textit{Anderson}, the finding does not matter: a seat in the House is a federal office, and States have no power to enforce Section~3 with respect to federal offices. Any disqualification must come through legislation enacted by Congress or the House's own power to judge its Members' qualifications, so the order must be reversed.
+
+\item \textbf{Opposite-Side (State Power Preserved): Barring a Former Sheriff From the Governor's Race.} In the same hypothetical, the State also acts against the former sheriff. By contrast, barring the former sheriff from the \emph{gubernatorial} ballot is constitutionally permissible under the Court's explicit carve-out preserving state power over state offices---subject, of course, to Fourteenth Amendment due-process constraints.
+
+\item \textbf{Opposite-Side (Likely Permissible): Excluding an Underage Presidential Candidate.} A 29-year-old files to appear on a State's presidential primary ballot, and the Secretary of State refuses because Article~II requires the President to be at least 35. The candidate invokes \textit{Anderson}'s holding that States may not enforce Section~3 against presidential candidates. \textit{Anderson} addressed only Section~3, a disability that requires fact-intensive findings and that Congress may remove by a two-thirds vote; an age requirement is objective, categorical, and cannot be waived by Congress, so state enforcement creates no risk of a conflicting state-by-state patchwork. Lower courts have allowed States to keep constitutionally ineligible candidates off the ballot, as in \textit{Hassan v.\ Colorado} (a naturalized citizen), so the exclusion is likely valid.
+
+\item \textbf{Fence-Sitter (Close Call): Enforcing the Two-Term Limit.} A former President who has already been elected twice files to run again, and a State keeps him off its presidential ballot under the Twenty-Second Amendment. Like the age requirement, the two-term limit is objective and turns on public records rather than disputed findings about conduct, and Congress has no power to waive it, which suggests that \textit{Anderson}'s concerns do not apply. On the other hand, \textit{Anderson} stressed the uniquely important national interest in presidential elections and the chaos of state-by-state ballot decisions, reasoning that could be read to bar any single State from deciding a presidential candidate's eligibility. Because the Court has never addressed the question, the outcome is uncertain.
+
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\section{Critique}
+(1) \emph{Textual Inversion:} Requiring affirmative congressional legislation allows a bare majority (or one chamber's inaction) to nullify Section~3, contradicting its text requiring a two-thirds supermajority of both Houses to lift a disability. (2) \emph{Selective Originalism:} Critics note that the Court relied heavily on consequentialist ``patchwork'' fears and a single lower-court precedent (\emph{Griffin's Case}) rather than original public meaning. (3) \emph{Unresolved Merits:} The Court left unanswered whether the President is an ``officer of the United States,'' what conduct constitutes ``insurrection,'' and whether Congress could reject electoral votes on Section~3 grounds during a joint session without prior Section~5 legislation.
+
+\section{Key Quotations}
 \begin{quote}
-``Because the Constitution makes Congress, rather than the States, responsible for enforcing Section 3 against federal officeholders and candidates, we reverse.'' \\
---- Per Curiam, \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/23-719}{\emph{Trump v. Anderson}, 601 U.S. 100 (2024)}
+``Because the Constitution makes Congress, rather than the States, responsible for enforcing Section 3 against federal officeholders and candidates, we reverse.''
 \end{quote}
+\noindent --- Per Curiam, \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/23-719}{\emph{Trump v. Anderson}, 601 U.S. 100 (2024)}.
 
 \begin{quote}
-``We conclude that States may disqualify persons holding or attempting to hold state office. But States have no power under the Constitution to enforce Section 3 with respect to federal offices, especially the Presidency.'' \\
---- Per Curiam, \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/23-719}{\emph{Trump v. Anderson}, 601 U.S. 100 (2024)}
+``We conclude that States may disqualify persons holding or attempting to hold state office. But States have no power under the Constitution to enforce Section 3 with respect to federal offices, especially the Presidency.''
 \end{quote}
+\noindent --- Per Curiam, \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/23-719}{\emph{Trump v. Anderson}, 601 U.S. 100 (2024)}.
 
 \begin{quote}
-``Nothing in the Constitution requires that we endure such chaos---arriving at any time or different times, up to and perhaps beyond the Inauguration.'' \\
---- Per Curiam, \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/23-719}{\emph{Trump v. Anderson}, 601 U.S. 100 (2024)}
+``Nothing in the Constitution requires that we endure such chaos---arriving at any time or different times, up to and perhaps beyond the Inauguration.''
 \end{quote}
+\noindent --- Per Curiam, \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/23-719}{\emph{Trump v. Anderson}, 601 U.S. 100 (2024)}.
 
 \begin{quote}
-``Particularly in this circumstance, writings on the Court should turn the national temperature down, not up. For present purposes, our differences are far less important than our unanimity: All nine Justices agree on the outcome of this case. That is the message Americans should take home.'' \\
---- Barrett, J., concurring in part and concurring in the judgment, \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/23-719}{Cornell LII}
+``Particularly in this circumstance, writings on the Court should turn the national temperature down, not up. For present purposes, our differences are far less important than our unanimity: All nine Justices agree on the outcome of this case. That is the message Americans should take home.''
 \end{quote}
+\noindent --- Barrett, J., concurring in part and concurring in the judgment, \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/23-719}{Cornell LII}.
 
 \begin{quote}
-``The Court today needed to resolve only a single question: whether an individual State may keep a Presidential candidate found to have engaged in insurrection off its ballot. The majority resolves much more than the case before us.'' \\
---- Sotomayor, Kagan, and Jackson, JJ., concurring in the judgment, \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/23-719}{Cornell LII}
+``The Court today needed to resolve only a single question: whether an individual State may keep a Presidential candidate found to have engaged in insurrection off its ballot. The majority resolves much more than the case before us.''
 \end{quote}
+\noindent --- Sotomayor, Kagan, and Jackson, JJ., concurring in the judgment, \href{https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/23-719}{Cornell LII}.
 
-\section{Doctrinal Significance and Modern Legacy (Through 2026)}
-
-\emph{Trump v. Anderson} stands as a landmark structural federalism decision alongside \emph{U.S. Term Limits, Inc. v. Thornton} and \emph{Anderson v. Celebrezze}, cementing a sharp asymmetry in constitutional election administration: States enjoy broad retained authority over state-office qualifications (including Section~3 disqualifications, as in New Mexico's 2022 ouster of county commissioner Couy Griffin; \href{https://constitution.heritage.org/essays/amdt-14-s3/}{Heritage Guide}), yet possess zero unilateral power to enforce Section~3 against federal candidates (\href{https://constitution.congress.gov/browse/essay/intro.9-2-7/ALDE_00000048/}{Constitution Annotated}).
-
-Immediately, the ruling overturned parallel ballot exclusions in Maine and Illinois, cleared Trump to appear on every 2024 primary and general-election ballot, and---paired with \emph{Trump v. United States}, 603 U.S. 593 (2024)---formed the legal backdrop to Trump's November 2024 election victory and January 2025 inauguration (\href{https://www.ussc.edu.au/constitutional-law-in-the-age-of-trump-2-0-the-supreme-court-s-2024-25-term-in-review}{USSC 2025 Review}). Through late 2026, Congress has enacted no new Section~5 civil enforcement statute, leaving 18 U.S.C.\ \S 2383 (criminal insurrection) and internal congressional seating powers under Article~I, \S 5 as the only active federal enforcement vehicles---rendering Section~3 largely dormant against federal candidates absent future congressional action.
-
-In constitutional scholarship from 2024 through 2026, \emph{Trump v. Anderson} sparked an intense methodological debate. Prominent originalists William Baude and Michael Stokes Paulsen argued in \href{https://harvardlawreview.org/print/vol-138/sweeping-section-three-under-the-rug-a-comment-on-trump-v-anderson/}{138 Harv.\ L.\ Rev.\ 676 (2025)} that the Court ``swept Section Three under the rug'' by inverting Article~II's state-centric Electors Clause and abandoning originalism for pragmatic consequentialism. Similarly, Ilya Somin contended in the \href{https://www.cato.org/sites/cato.org/files/2024-09/cato-supreme-court-review-2023-2024-14.pdf}{Cato Supreme Court Review (2024)} that the Court ignored States' routine enforcement of Article~II age and citizenship qualifications (such as then-Judge Gorsuch's Tenth Circuit opinion in \emph{Hassan v. Colorado}). Meanwhile, state-vs.-federal ballot-access boundaries continue to generate certiorari petitions into the 2025--2026 Term testing how far \emph{Trump v. Anderson} restricts state election officials from adjudicating presidential-elector and candidate eligibility (\href{https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/25/25-1244/407886/20260527090233541_20260527-085915-00001450-00003637.pdf}{No.\ 25--1244 Pet.\ for Cert.\ (2026)}).
-
-\section{Pedagogical Critiques and Exam Hypotheticals}
-
-\textbf{Key Critiques:} (1) \emph{Textual Inversion:} Requiring affirmative congressional legislation allows a bare majority (or one chamber's inaction) to nullify Section~3, contradicting its text requiring a two-thirds supermajority of both Houses to lift a disability. (2) \emph{Selective Originalism:} Critics note that the Court relied heavily on consequentialist ``patchwork'' fears and a single lower-court precedent (\emph{Griffin's Case}) rather than original public meaning. (3) \emph{Unresolved Merits:} The Court left unanswered whether the President is an ``officer of the United States,'' what conduct constitutes ``insurrection,'' and whether Congress could reject electoral votes on Section~3 grounds during a joint session without prior Section~5 legislation.
-
-\textbf{``Opposite Side'' Contrast (State vs.\ Federal Qualification Enforcement):} Contrast \emph{Trump v. Anderson} with \emph{Chiafalo v. Washington}, 591 U.S. 578 (2020), and \emph{Hassan v. Colorado}, 495 F.\ App'x 947 (10th Cir.\ 2012) (\href{https://www.lawfaremedia.org/article/what-the-supreme-court-got-wrong-in-the-trump-section-3-case}{Lawfare}). In \emph{Chiafalo}, the Court upheld broad state power under Article~II's Electors Clause to penalize ``faithless'' presidential electors, and lower courts have routinely permitted States to exclude underage or non-natural-born presidential candidates from ballots. On an exam, students should distinguish objective, categorical Article~II qualifications (age, citizenship, residency) and state-controlled elector mechanics from Section~3's fact-intensive, Congress-waivable, Reconstruction-era disability---analyzing whether \emph{Trump v. Anderson}'s anti-patchwork rationale is strictly limited to Section~3 or threatens state enforcement of the Twenty-Second Amendment's two-term limit.
-
-\textbf{Exam Hypothetical:} Suppose in 2028 a State Secretary of State excludes a former U.S.\ Senator from the U.S.\ Senate primary ballot after a state administrative hearing finds the candidate ``gave aid or comfort'' to a domestic rebellion, while simultaneously barring a former county sheriff from running for Governor on the exact same factual record. Under \emph{Trump v. Anderson}, the exclusion from the \emph{U.S.\ Senate} ballot is unconstitutional because States lack authority to enforce Section~3 against federal candidates; enforcement belongs exclusively to Congress (via Section~5 legislation or the Senate's Article~I, \S 5 judging power). By contrast, barring the former sheriff from the \emph{gubernatorial} ballot is constitutionally permissible under the Court's explicit carve-out preserving state power over state offices---subject, of course, to Fourteenth Amendment due-process constraints.
 
