-\section{Doctrinal Significance and Modern Legacy (Through 2026)}
-\textit{Chaplinsky} is the foundational source of the Supreme Court's categorical approach to the First Amendment, yet its two legacies have diverged sharply over eight decades: the ``fighting words'' doctrine itself has been relentlessly narrowed (the Supreme Court has never affirmed a fighting-words conviction since 1942), whereas \textit{Chaplinsky}'s categorical architecture---recast as a strictly historical inquiry---remains central to Roberts Court jurisprudence through 2026.
-\begin{itemize}
-  \item \textbf{Erosion of the Fighting Words Doctrine (\textit{Cohen} and \textit{Gooding}):} In \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/403/15/}{\textit{Cohen v. California}, 403~U.S.~15 (1971)}, the Court reversed a conviction for wearing a ``Fuck the Draft'' jacket in a courthouse, limiting fighting words to ``personally abusive epithets'' directed face-to-face at a specific individual rather than general profanity. One year later, in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/405/518/}{\textit{Gooding v. Wilson}, 405~U.S.~518 (1972)}, the Court struck down Georgia's ``opprobrious words or abusive language'' statute as facially overbroad because state courts had not restricted it strictly to words tending to incite an \textit{immediate} breach of the peace---effectively discarding \textit{Chaplinsky}'s first prong (``by their very utterance inflict injury''). Justice Powell's concurrence in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/415/130/}{\textit{Lewis v. City of New Orleans}, 415~U.S.~130 (1974)}, further suggested that police officers are trained to exercise higher restraint than ordinary citizens, casting doubt on whether Chaplinsky's own conviction would survive today.
-  \item \textbf{No Viewpoint Discrimination Within Fighting Words (\textit{R.A.V.}):} In \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/505/377/}{\textit{R.A.V. v. City of St. Paul}, 505~U.S.~377 (1992)}, the Court held that proscribable categories under \textit{Chaplinsky} are not ``entirely invisible to the Constitution'': government may not selectively ban only race- or religion-based fighting words while permitting other fighting words.
-  \item \textbf{From Value Balancing to Historical Categories (\textit{Stevens}):} In \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/559/460/}{\textit{United States v. Stevens}, 559~U.S.~460 (2010)}, Chief Justice Roberts rejected the government's effort to use \textit{Chaplinsky}'s cost-benefit language to create a new unprotected category for depictions of animal cruelty. Calling ad hoc value-balancing ``startling and dangerous,'' \textit{Stevens} reinterpreted \textit{Chaplinsky} as merely \textit{describing} a closed set of ``historic and traditional categories long familiar to the bar.''
-  \item \textbf{Mens Rea and Historical-Categories Enforcement (2023--2026):} In \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/600/22-138/}{\textit{Counterman v. Colorado}, 600~U.S.~66 (2023)}, the Court reaffirmed the \textit{Chaplinsky}/\textit{Stevens} list of historic exceptions (including fighting words and true threats) while holding that even within unprotected true threats, the First Amendment requires a subjective recklessness \textit{mens rea} to avoid chilling protected speech. Most recently, in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-539/}{\textit{Chiles v. Salazar}, 607~U.S.~\_\_\_ (Mar.~31, 2026)}, the Court again invoked the narrow \textit{Chaplinsky}/\textit{Stevens} canon (fraud, defamation, and fighting words) to reject a state's claim that professional counseling speech constitutes a historically unprotected category.
-\end{itemize}
-
-\section{Pedagogical Critiques and Exam Hypotheticals}
-\textbf{Critiques:} Modern scholars identify three major flaws in \textit{Chaplinsky}. First, its facts are notoriously ironic: Walter Chaplinsky was calling a city official a ``Fascist'' and ``racketeer'' during a heated dispute over police failure to protect an unpopular religious minority---core political criticism of government officials that modern doctrine (\textit{New York Times Co. v. Sullivan}, \textit{Cohen}, \textit{City of Houston v. Hill}) vigorously protects. Second, the Court's unexamined assumption that ``damned Fascist'' automatically triggers violence reflects an outdated, gender- and class-bound ``average addressee'' honor code that invites selective enforcement against unpopular speakers. Third, \textit{Chaplinsky}'s ``by their very utterance inflict injury'' prong collided with modern anti-censorship rules (\textit{Snyder v. Phelps}, \textit{Matal v. Tam}) holding that emotional distress alone cannot strip speech of First Amendment protection.
-
-\textbf{Exam Hypothetical:} During a heated sidewalk protest outside a city council meeting, an activist steps within two feet of a private counter-protester, clenches his fists, and screams a string of face-to-face personal profanities and a challenge to ``settle this right here in the street.'' The activist is arrested under a state statute---authoritatively construed by the state supreme court to reach only ``face-to-face words directly tending to provoke an immediate violent breach of the peace by the person addressed.'' He challenges his conviction under the First Amendment.
-\begin{itemize}
-  \item \textbf{State's Argument (Under \textit{Chaplinsky}):} Unlike the general political jacket in \textit{Cohen} or the un-narrowed statute in \textit{Gooding}, this statute carries an authoritative narrowing construction matching \textit{Chaplinsky}'s breach-of-the-peace prong, is content-neutral across all topics (avoiding \textit{R.A.V.}), and was applied to a direct, face-to-face personal provocation directed at a private citizen---not a trained police officer---in a manner likely to trigger immediate physical retaliation.
