+
+Justice White joined the Court's opinion while emphasizing that the Court continued to distinguish communicating by words from expressive conduct that impinges on valid state interests, and disclaiming endorsement of all dicta in \textit{Burnside v. Byars}.
+
+\subsection{Dissenting Opinion (Justice Black)}
+
+Justice Black issued a fierce dissent accusing the majority of resurrecting \textit{Lochner}-era ``reasonableness'' review and transferring control of America's 23,390 public school systems from elected officials to the federal judiciary and ``their loudest-mouthed, but maybe not their brightest, students.'' Justice Black denied that anyone has a First Amendment right to speak ``where he pleases and when he pleases'' (\textit{Cox v. Louisiana}, 379 U.S. 536 (1965)), argued that the armbands demonstrably diverted students' minds from classwork (noting a mathematics lesson reportedly disrupted by argument), and warned that undermining school discipline invited a ``new revolutionary era of permissiveness.''
+
+\subsection{Dissenting Opinion (Justice Harlan)}
+
+Justice Harlan agreed that public schools are bound by the Fourteenth Amendment, but argued that school officials deserve the ``widest authority'' to maintain order. He would have placed the burden on challengers to prove a school regulation was motivated by illegitimate viewpoint suppression rather than good-faith educational concerns, finding no evidence of bad faith on the record.
+
+\subsection{Subsequent Developments}
+
+\begin{itemize}
+
+\item \textbf{Three Categorical Carve-Outs (1986--2007):} Without overruling \textit{Tinker}, the Court held that schools may regulate (1) ``lewd, indecent, or offensive'' student speech at a school assembly in \textit{Bethel School District No. 403 v. Fraser}, 478 U.S. 675 (1986); (2) school-sponsored expressive activities (such as curricular student newspapers) for ``legitimate pedagogical concerns'' in \textit{Hazelwood School District v. Kuhlmeier}, 484 U.S. 260 (1988); and (3) speech reasonably viewed as promoting illegal drug use at a school-supervised event (a ``Bong Hits 4 Jesus'' banner) in \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/551/393/}{\textit{Morse v. Frederick}}, 551 U.S. 393 (2007).
+  \item \textbf{Off-Campus Social-Media Speech (\textit{Mahanoy Area School District v. B. L.}, 594 U.S. 180 (2021)):} In \href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/594/20-255/}{\textit{Mahanoy}}, an 8--1 Court held that a public high school violated the First Amendment by suspending a student from the cheerleading squad for a weekend, off-campus Snapchat post using profanity to vent about the school and team. While declining to bar schools categorically from regulating off-campus speech (e.g., severe bullying or threats), Justice Breyer identified three features---diminished \textit{in loco parentis} authority, the danger of 24-hour speech policing, and schools' role as ``the nurseries of democracy''---that heavily curtail school authority off campus and keep \textit{Tinker}'s demanding substantial-disruption standard intact.
+
+\end{itemize}
+
+\section{Examples: Future Applications}
+\begin{enumerate}
+
+\item \textbf{Same-Side (Student Wins): A ``Green Tyranny'' T-Shirt.} Following a polarizing local school-board vote on district climate policy, a 16-year-old public high school student wears a plain T-shirt to school reading ``Board Policy = Green Tyranny.'' She attends classes quietly, though two classmates argue loudly with her in the cafeteria during lunch, requiring a monitor to tell them to sit down. The principal orders her to turn the shirt inside out under a newly announced rule barring ``apparel criticizing school board policies,'' explaining that heated community protests over the vote might spill into the hallways. Meanwhile, students wearing ``Save Our Planet'' shirts are not disciplined. The T-shirt is passive, non-vulgar, non-school-sponsored political speech squarely governed by \textit{Tinker} (not \textit{Fraser}, \textit{Hazelwood}, or \textit{Morse}). A brief lunchtime argument resolved by a monitor falls far short of ``material and substantial interference,'' and the principal's generalized worry about community protests is precisely the ``undifferentiated fear or apprehension of disturbance'' \textit{Tinker} rejects. Moreover, permitting ``Save Our Planet'' shirts while banning anti-policy shirts mirrors Des Moines's unconstitutional viewpoint singling-out.
+
+\item \textbf{Same-Side (Student Wins): Lunchtime Leaflets About a City Council Meeting.} During the lunch period, a high school student hands classmates leaflets urging them to attend a city council meeting on local police policy. The leaflets are not vulgar, are not school-sponsored, and promote no illegal activity. A few students toss them aside; no class is interrupted. The principal suspends the student under a rule forbidding distribution of political material on campus, explaining that police policy is a divisive topic that could upset some families. Under \textit{Tinker}, the school must point to facts reasonably forecasting material and substantial interference with schoolwork or discipline, and a wish to avoid the discomfort of an unpopular viewpoint is not enough. Peaceful communication among students during lunch is the kind of intercommunication \textit{Tinker} treated as part of education, so the suspension violates the First Amendment.
+
+\item \textbf{Opposite-Side (School Prevails): Documented Disruption and a Viewpoint-Neutral Ban.} Change two facts in the T-shirt example: suppose (1) prior weeks at the same school saw physical altercations, walked-out classes, and repeated hallway confrontations specifically triggered by competing climate-policy apparel, and (2) the school enforces a viewpoint-neutral ban on \textit{all} apparel referencing the climate dispute (barring both ``Green Tyranny'' and ``Save Our Planet'' shirts) while allowing students to debate the issue in social-studies class and after-school clubs. Under those facts, the school has concrete, documented evidence reasonably forecasting ``material and substantial disruption'' rather than mere undifferentiated fear, and has eliminated viewpoint discrimination---bringing the restriction squarely within \textit{Tinker}'s permissible exception (and paralleling \textit{Blackwell v. Issaquena County Bd. of Ed.}, 363 F.2d 749 (5th Cir. 1966), distinguished by Justice Fortas in \textit{Tinker}).
+
+\item \textbf{Opposite-Side (School Prevails): Editing the Journalism Class Newspaper.} Students in a high school journalism class write an article for the class's newspaper, which is produced with school funds under a teacher's supervision, describing named classmates' experiences with family divorce. The principal removes the article before publication, concerned that the families were not given a chance to respond and that the material is inappropriate for younger readers. \textit{Tinker}'s disruption test does not govern. Under \textit{Hazelwood School District v.\ Kuhlmeier}, 484 U.S.\ 260 (1988), a school may control the content of school-sponsored expressive activities, such as a curricular newspaper, when its actions are reasonably related to legitimate pedagogical concerns. Because readers would attribute the newspaper to the school, the principal may edit it without proving any forecast of disruption.
+
+\item \textbf{Fence-Sitter (Unsettled): A Weekend Post Mocking a Teacher.} On a Saturday, from home, a student posts on social media an edited photo of a teacher with a crude, demeaning caption, which spreads quickly among classmates. On Monday, students giggle and pass the image around during the teacher's class, and the teacher reports feeling humiliated. The school suspends the student. Under \textit{Mahanoy}, off-campus speech receives strong protection because the school's \textit{in loco parentis} role is diminished there, yet the Court left room for schools to address serious bullying or harassment of particular people. The school can point to some in-class distraction and to \textit{Tinker}'s undefined concern for the rights of others; the student can answer that the post was off-campus mockery that caused discomfort rather than material and substantial disruption. How courts strike that balance remains uncertain.
+
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\section{Critique}
 \begin{itemize}
-  \item \textbf{Justice Stewart (Concurring):} Agreed with the judgment and much of the opinion, but rejected any ``uncritical assumption'' that children's First Amendment rights are coextensive with adults' rights, citing his concurrence in \textit{Ginsberg v. New York}, 390 U.S. 629 (1968), that children may lack the full capacity for individual choice presupposed by the First Amendment.
-  \item \textbf{Justice White (Concurring):} Joined the Court's opinion while emphasizing that the Court continued to distinguish communicating by words from expressive conduct that impinges on valid state interests, and disclaiming endorsement of all dicta in \textit{Burnside v. Byars}.
-  \item \textbf{Justice Black (Dissenting):} Issued a fierce dissent accusing the majority of resurrecting \textit{Lochner}-era ``reasonableness'' review and transferring control of America's 23,390 public school systems from elected officials to the federal judiciary and ``their loudest-mouthed, but maybe not their brightest, students.'' Justice Black denied that anyone has a First Amendment right to speak ``where he pleases and when he pleases'' (\textit{Cox v. Louisiana}, 379 U.S. 536 (1965)), argued that the armbands demonstrably diverted students' minds from classwork (noting a mathematics lesson reportedly disrupted by argument), and warned that undermining school discipline invited a ``new revolutionary era of permissiveness.''
-  \item \textbf{Justice Harlan (Dissenting):} Agreed that public schools are bound by the Fourteenth Amendment, but argued that school officials deserve the ``widest authority'' to maintain order. He would have placed the burden on challengers to prove a school regulation was motivated by illegitimate viewpoint suppression rather than good-faith educational concerns, finding no evidence of bad faith on the record.
+  \item \textit{The Heckler's Veto Problem:} Because \textit{Tinker} permits censorship when school officials reasonably forecast ``substantial disruption,'' hostile classmates can arguably silence unpopular speakers simply by threatening disorder---tensioning \textit{Tinker} against \textit{Terminiello} and standard First Amendment hostile-audience doctrine.
+  \item \textit{The Uncharted ``Rights of Others'' Prong:} Justice Fortas repeatedly paired ``material and substantial interference'' with ``collision with the rights of other students to be secure and to be let alone,'' yet never defined those ``rights.'' Modern courts split sharply over whether psychological discomfort or ideological offense counts as an invasion of peers' rights.
 \end{itemize}
 
