diff --git a/briefs/76_lujan_v_defenders_of_wildlife.tex b/briefs/76_lujan_v_defenders_of_wildlife.tex
index 9ef7eff..f60b139 100644
--- a/briefs/76_lujan_v_defenders_of_wildlife.tex
+++ b/briefs/76_lujan_v_defenders_of_wildlife.tex
@@ -83,27 +83,28 @@ Justice Blackmun argued that the majority misapplied the summary-judgment standa
 \section{Key Quotations}
 \begin{quote}
 ``Over the years, our cases have established that the irreducible constitutional minimum of standing contains three elements. First, the plaintiff must have suffered an `injury in fact'---an invasion of a legally protected interest which is (a) concrete and particularized, ... and (b) `actual or imminent, not ``conjectural'' or ``hypothetical,'' ' ... Second, there must be a causal connection between the injury and the conduct complained of---the injury has to be `fairly ... trace[able] to the challenged action of the defendant, and not ... th[e] result [of] the independent action of some third party not before the court.' ... Third, it must be `likely,' as opposed to merely `speculative,' that the injury will be `redressed by a favorable decision.' ''
---- Justice Scalia, Opinion of the Court, 504 U.S. at 560--61 (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/504/555/}{Justia}; \href{https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112747/lujan-v-defenders-of-wildlife/}{CourtListener}).
 \end{quote}
+\noindent --- Justice Scalia, Opinion of the Court, 504 U.S. at 560--61 (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/504/555/}{Justia}; \href{https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112747/lujan-v-defenders-of-wildlife/}{CourtListener}).
 
 \begin{quote}
 ``Such `some day' intentions---without any description of concrete plans, or indeed even any specification of when the some day will be---do not support a finding of the `actual or imminent' injury that our cases require.''
---- Justice Scalia, Opinion of the Court, 504 U.S. at 564 (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/504/555/}{Justia}; \href{https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112747/lujan-v-defenders-of-wildlife/}{CourtListener}).
 \end{quote}
+\noindent --- Justice Scalia, Opinion of the Court, 504 U.S. at 564 (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/504/555/}{Justia}; \href{https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112747/lujan-v-defenders-of-wildlife/}{CourtListener}).
 
 \begin{quote}
 ``To permit Congress to convert the undifferentiated public interest in executive officers' compliance with the law into an `individual right' vindicable in the courts is to permit Congress to transfer from the President to the courts the Chief Executive's most important constitutional duty, to `take Care that the Laws be faithfully executed,' Art. II, \S~3.''
---- Justice Scalia, Opinion of the Court, 504 U.S. at 577 (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/504/555/}{Justia}; \href{https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112747/lujan-v-defenders-of-wildlife/}{CourtListener}).
 \end{quote}
+\noindent --- Justice Scalia, Opinion of the Court, 504 U.S. at 577 (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/504/555/}{Justia}; \href{https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112747/lujan-v-defenders-of-wildlife/}{CourtListener}).
 
 \begin{quote}
 ``In my view, Congress has the power to define injuries and articulate chains of causation that will give rise to a case or controversy where none existed before, and I do not read the Court's opinion to suggest a contrary view.''
---- Justice Kennedy, concurring in part and concurring in the judgment, 504 U.S. at 580 (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/504/555/}{Justia}; \href{https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112747/lujan-v-defenders-of-wildlife/}{CourtListener}).
 \end{quote}
+\noindent --- Justice Kennedy, concurring in part and concurring in the judgment, 504 U.S. at 580 (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/504/555/}{Justia}; \href{https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112747/lujan-v-defenders-of-wildlife/}{CourtListener}).
 
 \begin{quote}
 ``In conclusion, I cannot join the Court on what amounts to a slash-and-burn expedition through the law of environmental standing.''
---- Justice Blackmun, dissenting, 504 U.S. at 606 (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/504/555/}{Justia}; \href{https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112747/lujan-v-defenders-of-wildlife/}{CourtListener}).
