----Chief Justice Roberts, Opinion of the Court, Part III--C, 567 U.S. at 574 (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/567/519/}{Justia}; \href{https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/809122/national-federation-of-independent-business-v-sebelius/}{CourtListener}).
+\noindent --- Chief Justice Roberts, Opinion of the Court, Part III--C, 567 U.S. at 574 (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/567/519/}{Justia}; \href{https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/809122/national-federation-of-independent-business-v-sebelius/}{CourtListener}).
 
 \begin{quote}
 ``In this case, the financial `inducement' Congress has chosen is much more than `relatively mild encouragement'---it is a gun to the head. \ldots\ The threatened loss of over 10 percent of a State's overall budget, in contrast, is economic dragooning that leaves the States with no real option but to acquiesce in the Medicaid expansion.''
 \end{quote}
----Chief Justice Roberts, Plurality Opinion, Part IV--A, 567 U.S. at 581--82 (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/567/519/}{Justia}; \href{https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/809122/national-federation-of-independent-business-v-sebelius/}{CourtListener}).
+\noindent --- Chief Justice Roberts, Plurality Opinion, Part IV--A, 567 U.S. at 581--82 (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/567/519/}{Justia}; \href{https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/809122/national-federation-of-independent-business-v-sebelius/}{CourtListener}).
 
 \begin{quote}
 ``The Chief Justice's crabbed reading of the Commerce Clause harks back to the era in which the Court routinely thwarted Congress' efforts to regulate the national economy in the interest of those who labor to sustain it.''
 \end{quote}
----Justice Ginsburg, Concurring in Part, Concurring in the Judgment in Part, and Dissenting in Part, Part I, 567 U.S. at 589 (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/567/519/}{Justia}; \href{https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/809122/national-federation-of-independent-business-v-sebelius/}{CourtListener}).
+\noindent --- Justice Ginsburg, Concurring in Part, Concurring in the Judgment in Part, and Dissenting in Part, Part I, 567 U.S. at 589 (\href{https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/567/519/}{Justia}; \href{https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/809122/national-federation-of-independent-business-v-sebelius/}{CourtListener}).
 
 \begin{quote}
 ``The Act before us here exceeds federal power both in mandating the purchase of health insurance and in denying non-consenting States all Medicaid funding. These parts of the Act are central to its design and operation, and all the Act's other provisions would not have been enacted without them. In our view it must follow that the entire statute is inoperative.''
 \end{quote}
----Justices Scalia, Kennedy, Thomas, and Alito, Joint Dissent, 567 U.S. at 648--49 (\href{https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/National_Federation_of_Independent_Business_v._Sebelius/Dissent_Scalia,_Kennedy,_Thomas,_and_Alito}{Wikisource}; \href{https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/809122/national-federation-of-independent-business-v-sebelius/}{CourtListener}).
+\noindent --- Justices Scalia, Kennedy, Thomas, and Alito, Joint Dissent, 567 U.S. at 648--49 (\href{https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/National_Federation_of_Independent_Business_v._Sebelius/Dissent_Scalia,_Kennedy,_Thomas,_and_Alito}{Wikisource}; \href{https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/809122/national-federation-of-independent-business-v-sebelius/}{CourtListener}).
+
 
 \end{document}
